/* Real researched culture packs, trimmed to the fields this prototype renders. */
export const PACKS = {"greek-orthodox":{"id":"greek-orthodox","name":"Greek Orthodox","shortName":"Greek Orthodox","faithGroup":"Christian","blurb":"The Service of Betrothal and the Service of Crowning in an Orthodox church, with the koumbaroi who crown you, the stefana, the common cup and the days of family customs either side.","typicalGuests":{"low":150,"typical":350,"high":1500},"terms":{"officiant":"priest","ceremonyVenue":"church","ceremony":"the crowning","bestMan":"koumbaros","maidOfHonour":"koumbara","reception":"reception","firstDance":"first dance","witnesses":"koumbaroi"},"events":[{"id":"engagement","name":"The engagement (arravones)","when":"Six to eighteen months before","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"The two families meet and the rings are exchanged, often blessed informally by a priest at home or in church. Plain gold bands are worn on the left hand from this point and are moved to the right hand by the priest during the wedding servic"},{"id":"invitations","name":"Delivering the invitations (ta prosklitiria)","when":"Two to four months before","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"Greek and Cypriot invitations are traditionally handed over in person rather than posted, by the couple, their parents or the koumbaroi, with a visit and a coffee at each house. In Cypriot villages the whole community counted as invited by "},{"id":"krevati","name":"Making the bed (to krevati)","when":"One to seven days before","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"Friends and unmarried female relatives make up the couple's marital bed together, usually three times, while the women sing. Guests then throw rice for roots, koufeta for sweetness and money for prosperity onto the bed, and a baby is rolled"},{"id":"resi","name":"Cooking the resi","when":"Two to three days before","optional":true,"default":false,"description":"A Cypriot custom, strongest in the Paphos and Limassol districts. Wheat is pounded with wooden flails, then cooked slowly for hours with lamb or goat to make resi, the dish served to the whole village at the wedding. It is a communal job sp"},{"id":"bachelor","name":"Stag and hen celebrations","when":"One to eight weeks before","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"A modern addition rather than a traditional one, and in Greece and Cyprus it is usually a night out rather than a weekend away. It does not replace the older customs of the eve and the dressing, which still happen."},{"id":"paramoni","name":"The eve (i paramoni)","when":"The night before","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"Each family hosts at home the night before, with food, wine and live music. In Cyprus this is the first of the three nights of a full traditional wedding and can run until the early hours. In much of the diaspora it has shrunk to a family d"},{"id":"stolisma","name":"The dressing (to stolisma / ta allamata)","when":"Wedding morning","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"The bride and groom are dressed at their parents' homes in front of family and friends, with violi and laouto players in the room. The groom is shaved by the koumbaros; the bride is dressed by the koumbara and her friends. In Cyprus, where "},{"id":"civil-registration","name":"Civil ceremony or registrar attendance","when":"Same day or shortly before","optional":true,"default":false,"description":"Whether you need this depends entirely on the country. In Greece, Cyprus, the United States, Canada and Australia the priest can register the marriage and no separate ceremony is needed. In England and Wales the church must be a registered "},{"id":"ceremony","name":"The Service of Betrothal and the Service of Crowning","when":"Wedding day","optional":false,"default":true,"description":"Two services celebrated back to back, historically separate. The betrothal blesses and exchanges the rings; the crowning places the stefana on your heads, joins your right hands, reads the wedding at Cana, gives you the common cup and leads"},{"id":"chairetisma","name":"The congratulations line (to chairetisma)","when":"Straight after the service","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"The couple, their parents and the koumbaroi stand in line and every guest passes to congratulate them. In Cyprus this is a distinct part of the day with its own much larger guest list: people who were not at the church and will not stay for"},{"id":"reception","name":"The reception","when":"Wedding evening","optional":false,"default":true,"description":"Dinner, the first dance, the money dance and then Greek dancing until very late. The order is looser than at a Western reception: speeches are short or absent, and the dancing starts early and does not stop. The couple's dance is followed b"},{"id":"antigamos","name":"The day after (o antigamos)","when":"The day after","optional":true,"default":false,"description":"A Cypriot custom. The families gather again the next day, often at the couple's or the parents' house, to eat the leftovers, count the money given and thank the people who worked on the wedding. In some villages it is called ta pkiata, afte"}],"items":[{"id":"church-offering","name":"Church offering","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"The donation to the parish for the use of the church. Most parishes publish a figure and it is usually higher if neither of you is a registered member or steward there. In the United States and the Un","essential":true,"share":0.012,"supplier":"Church","religious":true},{"id":"priest-offering","name":"Offering to the priest","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"A personal envelope for the celebrant, separate from the parish fee and normally handed over on the day. Allow for the preparation meetings as well as the service. If a priest travels to you, or if yo","essential":true,"share":0.006,"supplier":"Church","religious":false},{"id":"chanter-choir","name":"Chanter and choir","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"The psaltis chants the service. Some parishes include one in the fee, others charge separately, and a full Byzantine choir is a further cost again. Only chanting and, in some jurisdictions, an organ a","essential":true,"share":0.005,"supplier":"Church","religious":true},{"id":"church-permissions","name":"Certificates and permission to marry","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"The certificate of freedom to marry or celibacy for each of you, and the licence or permission issued by the Metropolis or Archdiocese. These carry small administrative fees and are charged per person","essential":true,"share":0.003,"supplier":"Church","religious":true},{"id":"civil-registration","name":"Civil registration and notice fees","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"Notice fees, licence fees and, where a registrar has to attend the church, the registrar's attendance fee. In Greece this covers the municipal marriage licence and the newspaper announcement; in Cypru","essential":true,"share":0.004,"supplier":"Registrar","religious":false},{"id":"stefana","name":"Stefana (wedding crowns)","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"The two crowns joined by a single white ribbon that is never cut. They are traditionally bought and brought by the koumbaros or koumbara, so agree early who is paying rather than buying two sets. Anyt","essential":true,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Church supplies","religious":true},{"id":"lambades","name":"Lambades (wedding candles)","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"The pair of tall decorated candles that stand either side of you during the service and are lit at the start of the crowning. Also usually provided by the koumbaroi. Some churches cap the height, so c","essential":true,"share":0.004,"supplier":"Church supplies","religious":true},{"id":"ceremony-tray","name":"Tray, goblet and wine","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"The dhiskos, the silver tray that carries the rings, the crowns and the koufeta to the altar, plus the goblet and the sweet wine for the common cup. Nama is normal in Greece and the diaspora, koumanda","essential":true,"share":0.003,"supplier":"Church supplies","religious":true},{"id":"koufeta-rice","name":"Koufeta, rice and petals for the church","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"Sugared almonds for the tray and to hand round after the service, and rice or petals for guests to throw. Check the rules first. Many parishes, especially in the United States and the United Kingdom, ","essential":true,"share":0.003,"supplier":"Church supplies","religious":false},{"id":"stefanothiki","name":"Stefanothiki (crown case)","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"The glazed case the stefana are kept in afterwards, traditionally hung on the bedroom wall above the bed. Bought after the wedding as often as before it, and sometimes the koumbaroi's gift.","essential":false,"share":0.003,"supplier":"Church supplies","religious":false},{"id":"church-flowers","name":"Church flowers and aisle decoration","section":"Flowers & Styling","event":"ceremony","note":"Arrangements for the entrance, the aisle and the table where the crowns and the Gospel sit. Orthodox churches are already highly decorated, so most couples do far less here than at a Western church we","essential":true,"share":0.025,"supplier":"Florist","religious":false},{"id":"venue-hire","name":"Reception venue hire","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"Cypriot and Greek weddings need a venue that can take a very large number of people standing for the chairetisma as well as seated for dinner, which rules out most conventional wedding venues. Village","essential":true,"share":0.065,"supplier":"Venue","religious":false},{"id":"catering","name":"Catering","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"The largest single line by a wide margin, and the one most affected by the guest count. Expect meze rather than a plated three-course meal in Greece and Cyprus: souvla, kleftiko, halloumi, koupepia, s","essential":true,"share":0.24,"supplier":"Caterer","religious":false},{"id":"drinks","name":"Drinks","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"Wine, beer, spirits and zivania or tsipouro. Open bar is the expectation at Greek and Cypriot weddings rather than a nice extra. Check corkage carefully: bringing your own wine and spirits is common a","essential":true,"share":0.07,"supplier":"Caterer","religious":false},{"id":"service-charge","name":"Service charge, corkage and staffing","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"Waiting staff, bar staff, corkage and cake-cutting fees. At a wedding of several hundred these add up to a meaningful line of their own and are frequently quoted separately from the per-head price.","essential":true,"share":0.012,"supplier":"Caterer","religious":false},{"id":"wedding-cake","name":"Wedding cake","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"Usually a tiered cake cut late in the evening. Greek and Cypriot receptions also put out trays of sweets alongside it, so the cake itself does not need to feed everyone.","essential":true,"share":0.014,"supplier":"Cake maker","religious":false},{"id":"traditional-dish","name":"Resi or traditional wedding dish","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"Resi in Cyprus, gamopilafo in Crete: a wheat or rice dish cooked in lamb or goat stock and served to everyone. If you want it made the traditional way rather than by the venue, it needs to be commissi","essential":false,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Caterer","religious":false},{"id":"late-food","name":"Late-night food","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"Greek and Cypriot receptions run past two in the morning and a second wave of food, usually souvla or soup, is normal rather than optional.","essential":false,"share":0.01,"supplier":"Caterer","religious":false},{"id":"reception-styling","name":"Table centrepieces and reception styling","section":"Flowers & Styling","event":"reception","note":"Centrepieces, linen, chair covers and any styling of the room. Because the guest count is high, this is priced per table and scales fast: cutting one flower from each centrepiece across a hundred tabl","essential":true,"share":0.03,"supplier":"Florist","religious":false},{"id":"bouquets","name":"Bouquets and buttonholes","section":"Flowers & Styling","event":"ceremony","note":"The bride's bouquet, which the groom traditionally hands to her at the church door rather than her carrying it in, plus buttonholes for the groom, the koumbaroi, the fathers and often a corsage for ea","essential":true,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Florist","religious":false},{"id":"band","name":"Live band","section":"Music & Entertainment","event":"reception","note":"A Greek band with bouzouki is the centre of the evening, not background music. Bands are usually booked for a set number of hours with overtime rates, and Greek and Cypriot receptions almost always ru","essential":true,"share":0.055,"supplier":"Band","religious":false},{"id":"dj","name":"DJ","section":"Music & Entertainment","event":"reception","note":"Often booked alongside the band rather than instead of it, to cover the breaks and to take the later part of the night.","essential":false,"share":0.018,"supplier":"DJ","religious":false},{"id":"violarides","name":"Traditional musicians for the dressing","section":"Music & Entertainment","event":"stolisma","note":"The violi and laouto players who come to the house for the stolisma or allamata, sing the traditional wedding songs and call each relative forward in turn. In Cyprus these are the violarides and the c","essential":false,"share":0.01,"supplier":"Band","religious":false},{"id":"dance-troupe","name":"Traditional dance troupe","section":"Music & Entertainment","event":"reception","note":"A folk dance group to open the dancing, common at larger Cypriot and Cretan weddings and at diaspora weddings where guests may not know the steps.","essential":false,"share":0.006,"supplier":"Entertainment","religious":false},{"id":"plates-flowers","name":"Flowers and plates for throwing","section":"Music & Entertainment","event":"reception","note":"Trays of flowers for guests to throw over the dancers, and plaster plates if your venue permits smashing. Many venues in Greece and Cyprus now allow flowers only. Check before you buy, and buy plaster","essential":false,"share":0.004,"supplier":"Entertainment","religious":false},{"id":"av-lighting","name":"Lighting, sound and staging","section":"Music & Entertainment","event":"reception","note":"Uplighting, a dance floor and a PA that can cover a very large room. Often bundled with the band or the venue, but worth pricing separately at scale.","essential":false,"share":0.018,"supplier":"AV hire","religious":false},{"id":"photographer","name":"Photographer","section":"Photos & Film","event":"","note":"Most Greek and Cypriot couples book from the stolisma in the morning through to the first dance, which is a long day. Ask whether the photographer has worked in an Orthodox church before: the priest c","essential":true,"share":0.06,"supplier":"Photographer","religious":false},{"id":"videographer","name":"Videographer","section":"Photos & Film","event":"","note":"Same constraints as the photographer. The Dance of Isaiah and the crowning are the two moments couples most regret losing, and both are short.","essential":true,"share":0.04,"supplier":"Videographer","religious":false},{"id":"album","name":"Album, prints and parents' copies","section":"Photos & Film","event":"","note":"Almost always bought after the wedding and almost always underestimated. Both sets of parents and the koumbaroi will expect an album.","essential":false,"share":0.007,"supplier":"Photographer","religious":false},{"id":"gown","name":"Bridal gown","section":"Attire","event":"","note":"Allow for the fact the dress is worn from the morning dressing at home right through to a very late night of dancing, and that money may be pinned to it. Shoulders are traditionally covered in church,","essential":true,"share":0.052,"supplier":"Bridal shop","religious":false},{"id":"bridal-accessories","name":"Veil, shoes, jewellery and wrap","section":"Attire","event":"","note":"The groom traditionally buys the bride's shoes, and the koumbaros puts coins inside them for luck. Unmarried friends write their names on the sole; whoever's name has worn away by the end of the night","essential":true,"share":0.012,"supplier":"Bridal shop","religious":false},{"id":"groom-outfit","name":"Groom's outfit","section":"Attire","event":"","note":"Suit, shirt, shoes and accessories. Note that the groom is dressed in front of an audience at the stolisma, so anything fiddly is worth practising.","essential":true,"share":0.02,"supplier":"Menswear","religious":false},{"id":"party-attire","name":"Koumbaroi and bridal party outfits","section":"Attire","event":"","note":"Who pays varies. In Greece and Cyprus attendants normally pay for themselves; in the American and Australian diaspora the couple more often contributes.","essential":false,"share":0.01,"supplier":"Bridal shop","religious":false},{"id":"hair-makeup","name":"Hair and make-up","section":"Beauty","event":"stolisma","note":"Booked to the house for the morning dressing rather than to a salon, because the stolisma happens at home with a room full of people watching. Price the trial and the travel separately.","essential":true,"share":0.016,"supplier":"Hair and make-up","religious":false},{"id":"family-beauty","name":"Hair and make-up for mothers and attendants","section":"Beauty","event":"stolisma","note":"At Greek and Cypriot weddings both mothers, the koumbara, the sisters and often the grandmothers are all done at the house on the morning. It is a bigger booking than couples expect and needs a team r","essential":false,"share":0.01,"supplier":"Hair and make-up","religious":false},{"id":"groom-shave","name":"Barber for the groom's shave","section":"Beauty","event":"stolisma","note":"The traditional shave is performed by the koumbaros, but most grooms now have a barber standing by to make it look right, with the koumbaros taking the first symbolic stroke.","essential":false,"share":0.003,"supplier":"Barber","religious":false},{"id":"invitations","name":"Invitations","section":"Stationery","event":"invitations","note":"Print more than you think. Cypriot lists in particular run far past the seated numbers because the chairetisma is separately and much more widely invited, and invitations are delivered by hand so extr","essential":true,"share":0.016,"supplier":"Stationer","religious":false},{"id":"bomboniere","name":"Bomboniera (favours)","section":"Stationery","event":"reception","note":"The small tulle or box favour holding koufeta, given to every guest. The number of almonds is always odd, usually five or seven, because the couple's life cannot be divided. One per household is norma","essential":true,"share":0.02,"supplier":"Stationer","religious":false},{"id":"wedding-cars","name":"Wedding cars","section":"Transport","event":"ceremony","note":"The bride travels from her parents' house to the church, and the couple from the church to the reception. In villages a short walking procession behind the musicians replaces the car altogether.","essential":true,"share":0.012,"supplier":"Car hire","religious":false},{"id":"guest-transport","name":"Guest transport","section":"Transport","event":"reception","note":"Coaches between the church and the reception, which is worth costing properly when the church is in a village and the reception is not. Also parking, which is the single most common complaint at large","essential":false,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Coach hire","religious":false},{"id":"rings","name":"Wedding rings","section":"Rings & Gifts","event":"ceremony","note":"Plain gold bands are the tradition, often the same rings worn on the left hand since the engagement and moved to the right hand by the priest during the betrothal. Greek and Cypriot couples wear the w","essential":true,"share":0.025,"supplier":"Jeweller","religious":false},{"id":"koumbaroi-gift","name":"Gifts for the koumbaroi","section":"Rings & Gifts","event":"","note":"The koumbaros and koumbara take on a lifelong role and traditionally pay for the stefana, the lambades and the tray themselves, so the gift is meaningful rather than token. An icon is the conventional","essential":true,"share":0.007,"supplier":"Gifts","religious":false},{"id":"family-gifts","name":"Gifts for parents and attendants","section":"Rings & Gifts","event":"","note":"For both sets of parents, the attendants and anyone who hosted the krevati or the stolisma.","essential":false,"share":0.007,"supplier":"Gifts","religious":false},{"id":"planner","name":"Wedding planner or coordinator","section":"Extras","event":"","note":"Worth pricing seriously at Greek and Cypriot scale. A day coordinator who can run a three-hour congratulations line, keep the food moving for six hundred people and manage a band that plays past two i","essential":false,"share":0.027,"supplier":"Planner","religious":false},{"id":"translations","name":"Document translation and apostilles","section":"Extras","event":"","note":"Needed when a baptismal certificate, birth certificate or divorce decree comes from another country. Greece requires certified translations into Greek by an approved translator; Cyprus accepts Greek o","essential":false,"share":0.004,"supplier":"Translation","religious":false}],"timeline":[{"mb":15,"task":"Speak to your parish priest before you book anything else, and ask which dates the church cannot marry you on","critical":true},{"mb":14,"task":"Check the fasting seasons against your shortlist: Great Lent and Holy Week, the Dormition fast in the first half of August, the Nativity fast, and the fixed days of 5 and 6 January, 29 August and 14 September","critical":true},{"mb":14,"task":"If either of you was married before, start the ecclesiastical divorce now, because it is separate from the civil divorce and takes months","critical":true},{"mb":13,"task":"Confirm the church is free and get the date written into the parish diary before you book a venue","critical":true},{"mb":12,"task":"Agree who the koumbaros or koumbara will be and confirm they are an Orthodox Christian in good standing, since only an Orthodox sponsor may exchange the rings and the crowns","critical":true},{"mb":12,"task":"Book the reception venue, checking its standing capacity as well as its seated capacity","critical":true},{"mb":11,"task":"Draw up the two guest lists you actually need: the church and dinner list, and the much larger congratulations list","critical":true},{"mb":11,"task":"Book the band, the photographer and the videographer, which go first at popular dates","critical":false},{"mb":10,"task":"Order or arrange the stefana and lambades with the koumbaroi, and agree who is paying for what","critical":false},{"mb":9,"task":"Start the paperwork: baptismal certificates for both of you, and certificates of freedom to marry or celibacy from your parish","critical":true},{"mb":8,"task":"If one of you is not Orthodox, confirm with the priest that the baptism is accepted and complete any declaration about raising children in the Orthodox faith","critical":true},{"mb":8,"task":"Book the caterer and confirm the per-head price, the minimum numbers and what happens if numbers rise","critical":false},{"mb":7,"task":"Book the violarides or traditional musicians for the morning dressing if you are having one","critical":false},{"mb":6,"task":"Begin the meetings with your priest and ask him to walk you through the service, including where you stand and what the koumbaros does","critical":true},{"mb":6,"task":"Order the invitations, allowing generously for the congratulations list and for hand delivery","critical":false},{"mb":5,"task":"In Cyprus, book the thalassaemia screening appointments for both of you and collect the certificates","critical":true},{"mb":4,"task":"Submit your documents to the priest so he can apply to the Metropolis or Archdiocese for permission to marry","critical":true},{"mb":4,"task":"Confirm the church's rules on photography, flowers, aisle runners, rice and confetti in writing","critical":false},{"mb":3,"task":"Start delivering the invitations in person, family by family","critical":false},{"mb":3,"task":"Give notice of marriage to the civil authority: the register office in the United Kingdom, the municipality in Greece, the Notice of Intended Marriage in Australia, the county clerk in the United States","critical":true},{"mb":3,"task":"In England and Wales, confirm whether the church is registered for marriages and book the registrar if one must attend","critical":true},{"mb":2,"task":"In Greece, publish the marriage announcement in a daily newspaper and collect the marriage licence from the parish or Metropolis","critical":true},{"mb":2,"task":"Confirm the ecclesiastical permission has come back from the Metropolis and check its expiry date against your wedding date","critical":true},{"mb":2,"task":"Book hair, make-up and the barber to come to the houses on the morning, for the mothers and attendants as well as the couple","critical":false},{"mb":1,"task":"Buy the koufeta, the wine for the common cup and the rice or petals, within the church's rules","critical":false},{"mb":1,"task":"Pay the church balance and put the priest's and chanter's envelopes together","critical":false},{"mb":1,"task":"Fix the date of the krevati with the friends who will make the bed, and the eve gathering with both families","critical":false},{"mb":1,"task":"Confirm running times with the band and agree the overtime rate in writing","critical":false},{"mb":0,"task":"Hold the stolisma and the eve gathering, and give the koumbaroi the stefana, lambades and tray to bring to the church","critical":false},{"mb":0,"task":"In the days after the wedding, register the marriage: within 40 days at the lixiarcheio in Greece, within 45 days with the Registrar in Cyprus","critical":true}],"paperwork":[{"item":"Orthodox baptismal certificate","who":"The Orthodox party, and often both","note":"Proof of baptism in the name of the Father, Son and Holy Spirit. If you were baptised abroad, request it early: a parish in Greece or Cyprus can take weeks or months to send one, and a certified translation may then be n"},{"item":"Baptismal certificate for a non-Orthodox partner","who":"The non-Orthodox party","note":"The Church will marry an Orthodox Christian to a baptised Christian of a Trinitarian church, and the certificate is how that is checked. Roman Catholic, Anglican, Lutheran, Methodist, Presbyterian, Armenian, Coptic and s"},{"item":"Certificate of freedom to marry or celibacy (pistopoiitiko agamias)","who":"Both","note":"Issued by your own parish, usually on the word of two witnesses who are not close relatives, confirming you have not married before. It expires, commonly after six months, so do not get it too early."},{"item":"Permission or licence to marry from the Metropolis or Archdiocese","who":"Both","note":"The priest applies on your behalf once your certificates are in. There is a fee and a processing time of anything from a week to a month or more. Nothing else in the church process can be finalised without it."},{"item":"Civil marriage licence or notice of marriage","who":"Both","note":"Varies entirely by country. Greece: a marriage licence from the municipality of your residence plus a marriage announcement published in a daily newspaper. Cyprus: freedom certificates and a notification. United States: "},{"item":"Thalassaemia screening certificate","who":"Both, in Cyprus","note":"The Church of Cyprus has required a premarital thalassaemia certificate since 1983 as part of the island's screening programme. It confirms you have been tested and counselled; it does not prevent you marrying."},{"item":"Ecclesiastical divorce decree","who":"Either party previously married in the Orthodox Church","note":"Separate from and additional to the civil divorce, and issued by the Metropolis. It usually has to be final at least a month before the new wedding, and it commonly takes far longer to obtain than couples expect."},{"item":"Civil divorce decree","who":"Either party previously married","note":"The final decree, not the provisional one."},{"item":"Death certificate of a former spouse","who":"Either party widowed","note":""},{"item":"Declaration that children will be baptised and raised Orthodox","who":"The non-Orthodox party, in most jurisdictions","note":"Required in the Australian and American archdioceses and normal elsewhere. Conversion of the non-Orthodox partner is not required, and marrying in the church does not by itself make them a member."},{"item":"Certificate of good standing for the koumbaros or koumbara","who":"The sponsor","note":"If the sponsor belongs to a different parish, their own priest must confirm in writing that they are an Orthodox Christian in good standing. A sponsor whose own marriage was never blessed by the Church, or who divorced w"},{"item":"Certificate of membership or stewardship","who":"Both, in the United States and Australia","note":"Parishes commonly check that at least one of you is a registered member, and the church fee is often materially higher if neither of you is."},{"item":"Passport or birth certificate","who":"Both","note":""},{"item":"Certified translations and apostilles","who":"Either party with foreign documents","note":"Greece requires translation into Greek by an approved translator. Cyprus accepts Greek or English. Allow weeks, not days."},{"item":"Registration of the marriage after the ceremony","who":"Both","note":"Greece: declare the marriage at the lixiarcheio within 40 days, or pay a fine. Cyprus: a certified copy must reach the Registrar within 45 days. In the United States, Australia and Canada the priest normally submits the "},{"item":"Registrar attendance or separate civil ceremony","who":"Both, in England and Wales","note":"An Orthodox church can only marry you legally if the building is registered for marriages and an authorised person or a registrar is present. Some parishes are set up for this, some are not, and where they are not the co"}],"customs":[{"name":"The koumbaros and koumbara","description":"Not a best man and maid of honour. The koumbaroi are sponsors of the marriage: they exchange the rings three times and the crowns three times during the service, they traditionally buy the stefana, the lambades and the tray, and they are conventionally asked to baptise your first child, which makes "},{"name":"The Service of Betrothal","description":"The first of the two services, historically performed at the engagement and now joined to the wedding. The priest blesses the rings, signs them three times over each of your foreheads beginning with the groom, and places them on your right hands. The koumbaros then exchanges them three times between"},{"name":"The stefana","description":"Two crowns joined by a single white ribbon, placed on your heads by the priest and exchanged three times by the koumbaros. They mark you as king and queen of your household, and they are also crowns of martyrdom, because a marriage asks both of you to give things up. The ribbon is never cut. Afterwa"},{"name":"No vows","description":"There are no vows and no spoken 'I do' in the Orthodox service. Marriage is understood as something God does rather than a contract the couple make, so the priest acts and the couple stand. Guests from other traditions are often surprised by this, and it is worth putting a line in the order of servi"},{"name":"The common cup","description":"After the Gospel reading about the wedding at Cana, you drink three times each from one cup of sweet wine. It is not communion. It stands for everything you will share from now on, the difficult as much as the good."},{"name":"The Dance of Isaiah","description":"The priest leads you three times around the table holding the Gospel and the cross, with the koumbaros following behind holding the ribbon that joins the crowns. These are your first steps as a married couple, and in many parishes guests throw rice at you as you go. Three hymns are sung, the first b"},{"name":"Rice and koufeta","description":"Rice for roots and fertility, koufeta for sweetness. Guests throw rice and petals as you leave the church, and every guest is given a bomboniera holding an odd number of sugared almonds, usually five or seven, because a marriage cannot be divided. The almond is hard, the sugar is sweet: the wish is "},{"name":"The dressing (to stolisma / ta allamata)","description":"The bride and groom are dressed at home on the wedding morning in front of family and friends, with violi and laouto players in the room. In Cyprus the clothes are first laid on a chair under a red cloth, incensed three times, and danced with by each relative in turn. Then comes the zosimo: the pare"},{"name":"The shaving of the groom","description":"The koumbaros shaves the groom, or at least takes the first stroke, as a public sign of trust. Friends then help him dress, each doing one thing, so that everyone has a part."},{"name":"The krevati","description":"Friends and unmarried female relatives make the marital bed, usually three times, then throw rice, koufeta and money onto it and roll a baby across it. The baby's sex is said to predict your first child's. In Cyprus the older form is the paploma, where the quilt is sewn and stuffed by hand with a re"},{"name":"The bride's shoes","description":"The groom traditionally buys the bride's shoes and the koumbaros puts coins inside them. Unmarried friends write their names on the sole before the wedding, and whichever name has worn away by the end of the night is the next to marry."},{"name":"The nifopsomo","description":"In parts of mainland Greece, a large wedding bread is broken over the bride's head as she leaves her parents' house and shared out among everyone standing there."},{"name":"Stepping on the foot","description":"When the epistle reaches the line asking the wife to respect her husband, guests watch the bride's feet: if she steps on the groom's foot first, she will run the house. It is a joke, and everyone in the church is in on it."},{"name":"The congratulations line (to chairetisma)","description":"The couple, both sets of parents and the koumbaroi stand in a line and every guest passes to congratulate them. In Cyprus this is the part of the day most people attend, it can last two or three hours, and it is where guests hand over the envelope. Wear shoes you can stand in."},{"name":"Money at the wedding","description":"Cash is the wedding gift, not an alternative to one. In Cyprus and much of Greece it is handed over in a named envelope at the congratulations line, and the amount is scaled to closeness, from a small sum for an acquaintance to a substantial one for family. The older form, still done during the coup"},{"name":"The zeibekiko","description":"A solo improvised dance, traditionally danced by one man alone to a slow heavy song, most often the groom or the bride's father. Nobody joins in. The people around him kneel in a circle and clap the rhythm, and cutting in is a serious insult. It is the emotional centre of the night rather than a par"},{"name":"The dances","description":"The couple dance first, then the parents and the koumbaroi lead in turn, then the floor opens. Expect kalamatianos and syrtos as the open circle dances everyone can join, tsamikos, tsifteteli, and hasapiko or sirtaki. Cyprus adds the sousta; Crete adds pentozali and its own sousta. The lead dancer i"},{"name":"Plate smashing","description":"More myth than tradition. Breaking plates was a feature of Athenian nightclub culture from the 1950s onwards rather than an old wedding rite, it was restricted by law under the junta, and in Greece it has largely been replaced by throwing trays of flowers over the dancers. It survives at some Greek-"},{"name":"The wedding ring hand","description":"Greek and Cypriot couples wear the wedding ring on the right hand. Plain gold bands are worn on the left from the engagement and the priest moves them to the right during the betrothal service. Much of the diaspora has quietly switched to the left hand, and Romanian Orthodox practice differs again."},{"name":"Delivering the invitations by hand","description":"Invitations are taken round in person by the couple, their parents or the koumbaroi, with a visit at each house. In Cypriot villages anyone from either family's village counted as invited without a card at all, and the historic invitation was a ring of sesame bread called a glistarka."},{"name":"The antigamos","description":"In Cyprus the families gather again the day after to eat what is left, count the money and thank the people who worked. In some villages it is called ta pkiata, after returning the borrowed plates."},{"name":"Avoiding a leap year","description":"There is a persistent folk belief in Greece and Cyprus that marrying in a leap year is unlucky, and marriage rates measurably dip in those years. It is superstition rather than Church teaching, and no priest will refuse you, but older relatives may raise it."}],"gotchas":["Set the date with the priest before the venue. Roughly a third of the year is closed to weddings between Great Lent and Holy Week, the Dormition fast in the first half of August, the Nativity fast, and fixed days including 5 and 6 January, 29 August and 14 September.","How much of the Nativity fast is closed varies by jurisdiction. The Greek Orthodox Archdiocese of America and many diaspora parishes restrict 13 to 25 December; the Church of Cyprus and much of Greece treat the whole fast from 15 November as closed. Ask your own priest rather tha","The Church of Cyprus does not marry on Wednesdays or Fridays outside the meat-eating periods, since both are fast days.","The koumbaros or koumbara must be an Orthodox Christian in good standing. A non-Orthodox partner cannot be the koumbaros of their own wedding, and a friend who is Orthodox but whose own marriage was not blessed by the Church, or who divorced without an ecclesiastical divorce, wil","An ecclesiastical divorce is a separate process from a civil divorce and is granted by the Metropolis, not a court. It routinely takes six months or more and the church wedding cannot be scheduled until it is final.","An Orthodox Christian may marry a baptised Christian of a Trinitarian church, but not an unbaptised person. If your partner is not baptised, the only route is baptism first, which needs its own preparation and time.","Certificates expire. Certificates of freedom to marry and the permission from the Metropolis are commonly valid for six months only, so obtaining them a year in advance means doing it twice.","In England and Wales an Orthodox wedding is only legally binding if the church building is registered for marriages and an authorised person or registrar is present. Some parishes are set up for this and some are not. Confirm in writing which applies to yours before you plan arou","In Greece the marriage must be declared at the lixiarcheio within 40 days or you pay a fine, and after 130 days the fine rises. In Cyprus a certified copy must reach the Registrar within 45 days.","Guest numbers are not really within your control at a Cypriot wedding. Because the congratulations line is openly invited and invitations are delivered by hand, several hundred people who never confirmed will come. Plan the standing capacity and the drinks for a number well above","Money given by guests is expected to cover much of the day, but it arrives on the night and most suppliers want paying before it. Cash flow, not total cost, is what catches Cypriot and Greek couples out.","Most parishes ban rice, almonds and confetti inside and outside the church, and some charge a cleaning fine. Ask what is permitted before you buy anything to throw.","Photographers and videographers are tightly constrained inside an Orthodox church. The priest decides where they may stand, several key moments happen with your backs to the congregation, and the post-service session may be capped at half an hour if another wedding follows.","Only chanting and, in some jurisdictions, an organ are allowed inside the church. Recorded music, string quartets and secular processional pieces are not, and 'Here Comes the Bride' is not used.","The service has no vows and cannot be personalised. Readings, hymns and the order are fixed, there are no personal readings or self-written promises, and requests to change it will be declined kindly but firmly.","A second or third marriage is celebrated in an abbreviated penitential form rather than the full service. A fourth is not permitted at all. If it is a first marriage for one of you, most priests will use the full service.","Spiritual kinship is a real impediment. Godparents cannot marry their godchildren or their godchildren's parents, and two people baptised by the same godparent cannot marry each other. First cousins and several in-law relationships are also barred.","Book the band by the hour and agree the overtime rate in writing. Greek and Cypriot receptions habitually run two or three hours past the contracted finish.","Bomboniera are conventionally one per household in Greece and Cyprus and one per person in much of the diaspora. Confirm which you are doing before ordering eight hundred of them.","In Cyprus both of you need a thalassaemia screening certificate before the Church will marry you. It is quick, but it is one more appointment to book and one more certificate to chase."]},"hindu":{"id":"hindu","name":"Hindu / South Asian","shortName":"Hindu","faithGroup":"Hindu","blurb":"Several days of haldi, mehndi and sangeet building to the vivaah under the mandap: the baraat, the sacred fire, the seven steps, the mangalsutra and sindoor, at a muhurat chosen with your pandit.","typicalGuests":{"low":150,"typical":300,"high":1000},"terms":{"officiant":"pandit","ceremonyVenue":"mandap","ceremony":"the vivaah","bestMan":"best man","maidOfHonour":"maid of honour","reception":"reception","firstDance":"first dance","witnesses":"witnesses"},"events":[{"id":"roka","name":"Roka and engagement (sagai / nischitartham)","when":"Three to eighteen months before","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"The formal agreement between the two families, and often the first party of the wedding. In North India the roka 'stops' the match and is followed by a ring ceremony (sagai or mangni); in the south the equivalent is the nischitartham or nic"},{"id":"ganesh-puja","name":"Ganesh puja and opening rituals","when":"One to seven days before","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"Most families open the wedding period with a puja to Ganesha, the remover of obstacles, held at home with close family. Regional forms sit alongside it: the graha shanti in Marathi and Gujarati families, the mandap or mandvo ceremony raisin"},{"id":"haldi","name":"Haldi (pithi / gaye holud)","when":"One or two days before, or the wedding morning","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"Family and friends apply a paste of turmeric, oil and water to the bride's and groom's skin to bless them and ward off ill fortune. Called pithi in Gujarati families and gaye holud in Bengali ones, it is playful and messy: everyone wears cl"},{"id":"mehndi","name":"Mehndi","when":"One or two days before","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"Henna is applied to the bride's hands and feet in intricate designs, with the groom's name or initials often hidden in the pattern for him to find later. Female relatives and friends have simpler designs done at the same time, with music an"},{"id":"sangeet","name":"Sangeet","when":"One or two nights before","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"A night of music and dancing, with choreographed performances by cousins and friends alongside open dancing. Originally a North Indian and Punjabi ladies' singing night, it has become a pan-Indian and diaspora fixture and is often the bigge"},{"id":"baraat","name":"Baraat, milni and welcome","when":"Wedding day, before the ceremony","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"The groom arrives in procession with his family and friends dancing around him, traditionally on a white horse and led by dhol drummers. At the entrance the milni greets the two families pair by pair, and the bride's mother welcomes the gro"},{"id":"ceremony","name":"The wedding ceremony (vivaah)","when":"Wedding day, at the muhurat","optional":false,"default":true,"description":"Held under the mandap and centred on the sacred fire, with the pandit chanting in Sanskrit. Core elements shared across most regions: the varmala exchange of garlands, kanyadaan, the fire offering (vivaha homa), the joining of hands, the ci"},{"id":"vidaai","name":"Vidaai (farewell)","when":"Straight after the ceremony or end of the day","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"The bride's formal farewell to her family as she leaves with the groom. In many North Indian families she throws handfuls of rice back over her head towards her parents' home as thanks for raising her. It is short and openly emotional, and "},{"id":"reception","name":"Reception","when":"Wedding evening or the day after","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"The large party: entrances, speeches, dinner and dancing. Traditionally hosted by the groom's family to introduce the bride to their community, and sometimes held in a different city for that reason, though many couples now hold a single co"},{"id":"griha-pravesh","name":"Griha pravesh (welcoming home)","when":"After the wedding","optional":true,"default":false,"description":"The bride is welcomed into the groom's family home for the first time as a wife, classically tipping over a vessel of rice at the threshold with her right foot and entering right foot first. A small family ritual rather than a hosted event,"},{"id":"civil-marriage","name":"Civil marriage or registrar attendance","when":"Before or after the religious ceremony, where required","optional":true,"default":false,"description":"Whether the religious ceremony is legally recognised depends on the country. In India the Hindu ceremony itself creates the marriage, which is then registered. In England and Wales the venue must be registered for marriages with an authoris"}],"items":[{"id":"pandit-fee","name":"Pandit's fee (dakshina)","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"The offering to the priest for conducting the ceremony, usually discussed openly in advance and handed over as dakshina on the day. Many families also book the same pandit for the Ganesh puja and othe","essential":true,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Pandit","religious":true},{"id":"muhurat-consultation","name":"Muhurat and horoscope consultation","section":"Ceremony","event":"","note":"The sitting with a pandit or astrologer to fix the muhurat, the auspicious date and exact time for the ceremony, worked out from the panchang (Hindu almanac) and often from the couple's birth details.","essential":true,"share":0.002,"supplier":"Pandit","religious":false},{"id":"ceremony-venue","name":"Ceremony venue hire","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"A temple hall, banquet hall, marriage hall (kalyana mandapam in the south) or outdoor space that can hold the mandap and the guest count. Two things rule venues out fast: whether they permit an open f","essential":true,"share":0.055,"supplier":"Venue","religious":false},{"id":"mandap","name":"Mandap hire and decoration","section":"Flowers & Styling","event":"ceremony","note":"The four-pillared canopy under which the ceremony happens, its pillars often read as the four parents or the four aims of life. Usually hired as a decorated structure with seating for the couple, both","essential":true,"share":0.045,"supplier":"Mandap and decor","religious":true},{"id":"havan-samagri","name":"Puja items and havan samagri","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"Everything the rituals consume: the samagri (herbs and woods) and ghee for the fire, coconuts, rice, kumkum, turmeric, betel leaves, the kalash, flowers and the parched rice for the offerings. Your pa","essential":true,"share":0.005,"supplier":"Pandit","religious":true},{"id":"varmala-garlands","name":"Varmala garlands","section":"Flowers & Styling","event":"ceremony","note":"The two fresh flower garlands the couple exchange at the start of the ceremony. They are handled, lifted and photographed constantly, so they are made heavier and fresher than ordinary garlands; in th","essential":true,"share":0.003,"supplier":"Florist","religious":true},{"id":"civil-registration","name":"Civil registration and licence fees","section":"Ceremony","event":"civil-marriage","note":"Varies entirely by country: the marriage licence in the United States and Canada, notice of marriage for each of you in England and Wales plus any registrar attendance fee, the Notice of Intended Marr","essential":true,"share":0.003,"supplier":"Registrar","religious":false},{"id":"reception-venue","name":"Reception venue hire","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"Needs genuine capacity for a large seated dinner plus a stage and dance floor, and ideally tolerance for a late finish. If the ceremony and reception are in one venue, check the turnaround time to str","essential":true,"share":0.07,"supplier":"Venue","religious":false},{"id":"catering-wedding-day","name":"Wedding day catering","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"The biggest line in the budget. Menus are regional: North Indian, Gujarati thali service, South Indian meals traditionally served on banana leaves, Indo-Caribbean, or a mix when the families differ. B","essential":true,"share":0.22,"supplier":"Caterer","religious":false},{"id":"catering-pre-events","name":"Catering for haldi, mehndi and sangeet","section":"Reception","event":"","note":"The pre-wedding events all feed people too, and across three or four gatherings this quietly becomes one of the larger lines. Home-cooked food and chaat stations are common for haldi and mehndi; the s","essential":true,"share":0.05,"supplier":"Caterer","religious":false},{"id":"drinks","name":"Drinks","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"Practice varies more here than anywhere else. Alcohol is never served at the ceremony, temple venues ban it from the premises entirely, and some families are dry throughout; others run a full bar at t","essential":true,"share":0.028,"supplier":"Caterer","religious":false},{"id":"service-staffing","name":"Service charge and staffing","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"Waiting and bar staff, buffet and live-station staffing, and cleanup, often quoted separately from the per-head food price. Live counters (dosa, chaat, tandoor) each carry their own staffing cost.","essential":true,"share":0.01,"supplier":"Caterer","religious":false},{"id":"wedding-cake","name":"Wedding cake","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"Not a Hindu tradition, but a common diaspora addition for the reception. If your families are strictly vegetarian, order an eggless cake; most Indian bakers do this as standard.","essential":false,"share":0.005,"supplier":"Cake maker","religious":false},{"id":"mithai-favours","name":"Mithai and favours","section":"Stationery","event":"","note":"Boxes of Indian sweets given to guests, and larger trays exchanged between the families across the week. Many families also send sweets out with the invitations to close relatives. Order per household","essential":true,"share":0.01,"supplier":"Caterer","religious":false},{"id":"bridal-outfit","name":"Bridal lehenga or sari","section":"Attire","event":"ceremony","note":"Red and deep jewel tones are traditional for the ceremony in much of India, though regional dress differs beautifully: a lehenga or red sari in the north, a Kanjeevaram silk sari in Tamil families, a ","essential":true,"share":0.045,"supplier":"Bridal wear","religious":false},{"id":"bridal-additional-outfits","name":"Bride's outfits for the other events","section":"Attire","event":"","note":"A separate look for the mehndi, sangeet and reception is now standard: typically lighter lehengas or suits for the pre-events and a gown or second lehenga for the reception. This multiplies quickly, a","essential":false,"share":0.02,"supplier":"Bridal wear","religious":false},{"id":"groom-sherwani","name":"Groom's sherwani or ceremony outfit","section":"Attire","event":"ceremony","note":"A sherwani with a safa (turban) and often a kalgi brooch in the north; a kurta with dhoti, or a veshti with angavastram, in southern and eastern traditions where the groom may also be bare-chested wit","essential":true,"share":0.015,"supplier":"Menswear","religious":false},{"id":"groom-additional-outfits","name":"Groom's outfits for the other events","section":"Attire","event":"","note":"Kurtas for the haldi and mehndi, something sharper for the sangeet, a suit or second sherwani for the reception. Simpler and cheaper than the bride's set, but still several outfits.","essential":false,"share":0.007,"supplier":"Menswear","religious":false},{"id":"family-outfits","name":"Outfits for parents and close family","section":"Attire","event":"","note":"Both sets of parents and siblings are on show all week and in every photograph, and in many families the couple or parents fund matched outfits for the immediate family, particularly for the wedding d","essential":false,"share":0.012,"supplier":"Bridal wear","religious":false},{"id":"safa-turbans","name":"Safas for the baraat","section":"Attire","event":"baraat","note":"In many North Indian and Rajasthani-style weddings, male guests on the groom's side wear matching turbans for the baraat, hired in bulk with a tier ready to tie them on the day.","essential":false,"share":0.003,"supplier":"Menswear","religious":false},{"id":"mangalsutra","name":"Mangalsutra or thali","section":"Rings & Gifts","event":"ceremony","note":"The necklace the groom ties or fastens on the bride at the heart of the ceremony: black beads and gold in most of India, the thali or mangalyam on a turmeric-rubbed thread tied with three knots in Tam","essential":true,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Jeweller","religious":true},{"id":"gold-jewellery","name":"Bridal gold and jewellery","section":"Rings & Gifts","event":"","note":"Usually the second-largest line after catering. The bridal set for the ceremony, pieces for the other events, and gold given by both families, traditionally regarded as the bride's own property (strid","essential":true,"share":0.08,"supplier":"Jeweller","religious":false},{"id":"rings","name":"Engagement and wedding rings","section":"Rings & Gifts","event":"roka","note":"Ring exchange belongs to the engagement (sagai) rather than the wedding ceremony itself, which centres on the mangalsutra instead. Many couples add a Western-style ring exchange at the ceremony or rec","essential":true,"share":0.01,"supplier":"Jeweller","religious":false},{"id":"family-gifts","name":"Gifts between the families (shagun)","section":"Rings & Gifts","event":"","note":"Trays of saris, suit lengths, dried fruit, sweets and shagun envelopes flow between the two families at the roka, the wedding and after. The scale varies enormously by family and region; what matters ","essential":false,"share":0.015,"supplier":"Gifts","religious":false},{"id":"sangeet-entertainment","name":"Sangeet DJ, sound and staging","section":"Music & Entertainment","event":"sangeet","note":"The sangeet needs a proper sound system, microphones for performances, a stage or clear floor and someone to run the playlist between acts. A DJ who knows Bollywood, bhangra and your regional music wi","essential":true,"share":0.015,"supplier":"DJ","religious":false},{"id":"choreographer","name":"Sangeet choreographer","section":"Music & Entertainment","event":"sangeet","note":"Hired to drill the family dance performances in the weeks before. Common wherever the sangeet is a big production; rehearsals over video call with relatives abroad are now routine.","essential":false,"share":0.004,"supplier":"Entertainment","religious":false},{"id":"dhol-baraat","name":"Dhol players for the baraat","section":"Music & Entertainment","event":"baraat","note":"Live dhol drummers lead the baraat and set its energy; a brass band (baaja) is the fuller traditional form in India. Book for a set duration and agree the route, because a baraat with good dhol player","essential":false,"share":0.006,"supplier":"Band","religious":false},{"id":"ceremony-musicians","name":"Ceremony musicians (shehnai or nadaswaram)","section":"Music & Entertainment","event":"ceremony","note":"The shehnai is the classic auspicious sound of a North Indian wedding; in Tamil and Telugu weddings the nadaswaram and thavil drums are close to essential, with the getti melam crescendo masking any i","essential":false,"share":0.004,"supplier":"Band","religious":false},{"id":"reception-music","name":"Reception DJ or band","section":"Music & Entertainment","event":"reception","note":"Usually a DJ with an emcee who can manage entrances, speeches and an open floor across generations and possibly languages. Live Bollywood bands and fusion acts are popular where budgets allow.","essential":true,"share":0.02,"supplier":"DJ","religious":false},{"id":"av-lighting","name":"Lighting, sound and staging","section":"Music & Entertainment","event":"","note":"Uplighting, stage wash for the mandap and sangeet performances, projection for photo montages, and a PA that reaches the back of a big room. Often spread across several events, so price it as a packag","essential":false,"share":0.012,"supplier":"AV hire","religious":false},{"id":"photographer","name":"Photographer","section":"Photos & Film","event":"","note":"A multi-day booking covering haldi, mehndi, sangeet, the ceremony and reception, usually with a second shooter so the bride's and groom's preparations are both covered. Choose someone who knows Hindu ","essential":true,"share":0.05,"supplier":"Photographer","religious":false},{"id":"videographer","name":"Videographer","section":"Photos & Film","event":"","note":"Same multi-day scope as the photographer, and same need for ceremony experience. Highlight films and same-day edits shown at the reception are popular; livestreaming for relatives abroad has become a ","essential":true,"share":0.035,"supplier":"Videographer","religious":false},{"id":"album","name":"Albums and parents' copies","section":"Photos & Film","event":"","note":"Both sets of parents will expect an album. Ordered after the wedding and routinely underestimated.","essential":false,"share":0.005,"supplier":"Photographer","religious":false},{"id":"bridal-hmua","name":"Bridal hair and make-up","section":"Beauty","event":"","note":"A multi-event booking: distinct looks for mehndi, sangeet, the ceremony and reception, with the wedding-day look built around the outfit, jewellery and possibly a dupatta set over the hair. If the muh","essential":true,"share":0.012,"supplier":"Hair and make-up","religious":false},{"id":"mehndi-artist","name":"Mehndi artist","section":"Beauty","event":"mehndi","note":"Bridal mehndi covering hands to forearms and feet takes three to eight hours, so it happens a day or two before the wedding and the artist is booked for the full session. Additional artists are usuall","essential":true,"share":0.006,"supplier":"Mehndi artist","religious":false},{"id":"family-beauty","name":"Hair and make-up for mothers and close family","section":"Beauty","event":"","note":"Both mothers, sisters and often grandmothers book professional hair and make-up for the wedding day and reception, plus sari draping, which is a genuine skill worth paying for. It needs a team, not on","essential":false,"share":0.006,"supplier":"Hair and make-up","religious":false},{"id":"invitations","name":"Invitations","section":"Stationery","event":"","note":"Traditionally opened with an invocation to Ganesha and naming both sets of parents and grandparents as hosts, with separate inserts per event since the guest lists differ. Elders often hand-deliver th","essential":true,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Stationer","religious":false},{"id":"baraat-transport","name":"Baraat horse or car","section":"Transport","event":"baraat","note":"The decorated white horse (ghodi) is traditional in the north; a vintage or luxury car is the common substitute, and some grooms arrive by other means entirely. Horse hire comes with handler, welfare ","essential":false,"share":0.005,"supplier":"Car hire","religious":false},{"id":"guest-transport","name":"Wedding cars and guest transport","section":"Transport","event":"","note":"The couple's cars across multiple days and venues, plus coaches for guests where the ceremony and reception are apart. The vidaai car is decorated for the couple's departure. With events across severa","essential":true,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Car hire","religious":false},{"id":"florals-styling","name":"Florals and venue styling","section":"Flowers & Styling","event":"","note":"Beyond the mandap: entrance and stage decor, table centrepieces, marigold garlands and strings which are the signature flower of Indian weddings, and themed decor for the mehndi and sangeet. Across fo","essential":true,"share":0.03,"supplier":"Florist","religious":false},{"id":"planner","name":"Wedding planner or coordinator","section":"Extras","event":"","note":"Coordinating five events, two families, a pandit, a muhurat-fixed timeline and several hundred guests is a serious logistics job. A planner who has run Hindu weddings, and ideally your region's style ","essential":false,"share":0.025,"supplier":"Planner","religious":false},{"id":"guest-accommodation","name":"Accommodation for out-of-town family","section":"Extras","event":"","note":"Hindu weddings pull family across countries, and hosts traditionally help house close relatives for the whole event span, not one night. Negotiate a hotel block early and decide which rooms the famili","essential":false,"share":0.02,"supplier":"Hotel","religious":false}],"timeline":[{"mb":14,"task":"Sit down with your pandit or astrologer and fix the muhurat before you book anything else, since the auspicious date and time anchor every other booking","critical":true},{"mb":14,"task":"Ask which periods are closed for weddings in your community's calendar, such as Chaturmas, adhik maas and periods when Venus or Jupiter is combust, so you know which months are simply unavailable","critical":true},{"mb":13,"task":"Agree between the two families who is hosting and paying for what, event by event, before the big bookings are made","critical":true},{"mb":13,"task":"Book the ceremony and reception venues, confirming in writing that they allow an open flame for the havan and can open at the hour your muhurat requires","critical":true},{"mb":12,"task":"Book the pandit for the wedding date and the supporting pujas, since good pandits are booked out on the same auspicious dates as the venues","critical":true},{"mb":12,"task":"Draw up guest lists per event with both families, since the ceremony, sangeet and reception lists usually differ","critical":true},{"mb":11,"task":"Book the photographer and videographer for the full multi-day span, not just the wedding day","critical":false},{"mb":10,"task":"Book a caterer experienced in Indian and fully vegetarian cooking, and agree the approach to eggs, Jain and vegan requirements and alcohol with both families","critical":true},{"mb":10,"task":"Book the mandap and decor company and connect them directly with the venue about the fire","critical":false},{"mb":9,"task":"Start outfit shopping for the couple, allowing for a buying trip to India or long lead times on made-to-order lehengas and sherwanis","critical":false},{"mb":9,"task":"Begin jewellery purchases, spreading the gold buying over the months rather than one late purchase","critical":false},{"mb":8,"task":"Book the sangeet DJ, dhol players, ceremony musicians and, if you want one, the choreographer","critical":false},{"mb":8,"task":"Book the mehndi artist and the hair and make-up artist for all events, confirming they can start before dawn if the muhurat is early","critical":false},{"mb":7,"task":"Reserve hotel blocks for out-of-town family across the full event span","critical":false},{"mb":6,"task":"Order invitations with separate event inserts, and send save-the-dates to overseas guests who need visas and flights","critical":false},{"mb":6,"task":"Fix the schedule of pre-wedding events with both families, including who hosts the haldi, mehndi and sangeet and where","critical":false},{"mb":5,"task":"Check the legal position where you are marrying: whether the pandit can solemnise legally, whether the venue is registered, and whether you need a separate civil ceremony","critical":true},{"mb":4,"task":"Walk through the ceremony with your pandit: which rituals are included, how long it will run, who performs the kanyadaan and the other family roles, and whether he will explain the rituals in English as he goes","critical":true},{"mb":4,"task":"Collect the samagri list from the pandit and agree who is sourcing the puja items","critical":false},{"mb":3,"task":"Give legal notice where required: notice of marriage in the United Kingdom, the Notice of Intended Marriage in Australia, the marriage licence application window in the United States or Canada","critical":true},{"mb":3,"task":"Plan the baraat route with the venue and check whether a street procession or horse needs council or police permission","critical":false},{"mb":3,"task":"Hold the menu tasting and fix the final menus per event","critical":false},{"mb":2,"task":"Confirm the exact muhurat timing with the pandit and build the wedding day run sheet backwards from it","critical":true},{"mb":2,"task":"Start sangeet rehearsals and confirm the performance running order","critical":false},{"mb":1,"task":"Chase RSVPs per event, set the seating plan and order the mithai boxes per household","critical":false},{"mb":1,"task":"Confirm every vendor against the run sheet, and order the fresh garlands and florals for delivery on the day","critical":false},{"mb":1,"task":"Write a short ceremony program explaining the rituals for guests who have not attended a Hindu wedding before","critical":false},{"mb":0,"task":"Hold the Ganesh puja, haldi, mehndi and sangeet on the planned days, keeping the bridal mehndi session clear of other commitments","critical":false},{"mb":0,"task":"After the wedding, register the marriage and obtain the certificate: with the state registrar in India, or confirm the licence or schedule has been returned where the pandit solemnised legally","critical":true}],"paperwork":[{"item":"Civil marriage licence or notice of marriage","who":"Both","note":"Varies by country. United States and Canada: a licence from the county or province, which the pandit signs if authorised. Australia: a Notice of Intended Marriage at least one calendar month before. England and Wales: no"},{"item":"Confirmation the pandit can legally solemnise","who":"The couple","note":"In the United States, Canada and Australia many pandits are authorised or registered officiants, but not all are, and some cities require officiant registration. If yours is not, plan a short civil ceremony and let the r"},{"item":"Separate civil ceremony where the religious one has no legal effect","who":"Both","note":"In England and Wales a Hindu ceremony in an unregistered venue, including most hotels and marquees, has no legal effect on its own. Many couples marry at a register office before the wedding week, or book a registrar int"},{"item":"Marriage registration in India","who":"Both, if marrying in India","note":"The ceremony itself creates the marriage under the Hindu Marriage Act, but the Supreme Court has directed that marriages be registered, state rules set the procedure, and the certificate is what banks, passport offices a"},{"item":"Proof of age and identity","who":"Both","note":"Birth certificate or passport. In India the legal minimum ages are 21 for the groom and 18 for the bride under the Hindu Marriage Act."},{"item":"Divorce decree absolute","who":"Either party previously married","note":"The final decree, for both the civil authorities and the pandit, who will usually ask."},{"item":"Death certificate of a former spouse","who":"Either party widowed","note":""},{"item":"Special Marriage Act route for interfaith couples in India","who":"Couples where one partner is not Hindu, Sikh, Jain or Buddhist","note":"The Hindu Marriage Act covers marriages where both partners are Hindu, Sikh, Jain or Buddhist. An interfaith couple in India marries under the Special Marriage Act instead, which requires 30 days' public notice at the ma"},{"item":"Temple booking and religious requirements","who":"Both, if marrying at a temple","note":"Temples have their own forms and rules: advance booking, sometimes proof or declaration that both partners are Hindu or of dharmic faiths, and strict premises rules on food and alcohol. Ask early rather than assuming."},{"item":"The pandit's samagri list","who":"The couple and both families","note":"Not legal paperwork, but treat it like it: the itemised list of puja materials the ceremony needs, issued by the pandit. Losing track of it, or of who is bringing what, is a classic wedding-morning scramble."},{"item":"Apostilles and certified translations","who":"Either party with foreign documents","note":"Needed when documents cross borders: a couple marrying in India with foreign birth or divorce certificates, or a couple abroad registering an Indian marriage certificate for immigration. Allow weeks, not days."},{"item":"Marriage certificate for immigration","who":"Couples living across borders","note":"Spousal visa applications scrutinise the marriage certificate and evidence of the ceremony. If your wedding is in India and your life is elsewhere, register promptly and keep photographs, the invitation and the certifica"}],"customs":[{"name":"The muhurat","description":"The auspicious date and exact time for the ceremony, calculated by a pandit or astrologer from the panchang, the Hindu almanac, using the lunar day, the nakshatra and the ascendant, and often the couple's birth charts. The key ritual moment is timed to it precisely, whatever hour it falls at. It is "},{"name":"Kundali matching","description":"In many families the couple's birth charts are compared (gun milan) before the engagement is fixed, scoring compatibility across a set of factors. How seriously it is taken varies from decisive to ceremonial; remedies exist within the tradition for almost every flagged mismatch, which tells you how "},{"name":"The mandap","description":"The four-pillared canopy the ceremony happens under, a temporary sacred space built wherever the wedding is. The four pillars are widely read as the four parents whose care brought the couple to this day, or the four aims of life. The couple, their parents and the pandit sit within it around the fir"},{"name":"Agni, the sacred fire","description":"The fire at the centre of the ceremony is the divine witness to the marriage. Offerings of ghee and herbs are made into it through the vivaha homa, and the vows and circuits all happen before it. This is why a Hindu ceremony cannot simply relocate into any venue: the fire, even a modest contained on"},{"name":"The baraat and milni","description":"The groom's noisy, joyful procession to the venue, traditionally on a white horse with dhol drummers, family dancing around him. At the door the milni introduces the two families pair by pair with garlands and embraces. Strongest in North Indian and diaspora practice; most South Indian weddings welc"},{"name":"Varmala (jaimala)","description":"The couple exchange fresh flower garlands as first acceptance of each other, usually to loud cheering, with each side sometimes lifting their candidate to make the other's reach comically difficult. In Bengali weddings this is mala badal, done after the subho drishti, the bride and groom's ritual fi"},{"name":"Kanyadaan","description":"The bride's parents formally give their daughter's hand into the groom's, among the most emotional moments of the ceremony and traditionally counted a great act of merit for the parents. Some modern couples rework or soften it, preferring language of blessing over giving; pandits differ on how flexi"},{"name":"Panigrahana and gathbandhan","description":"The groom takes the bride's hand before the fire, accepting responsibility for the life they will build, and their garments are knotted together, the gathbandhan, so they move as one for the rituals that follow. In Gujarati and Punjabi families the joining of hands is called hast milap."},{"name":"Mangal pheras","description":"The couple walk in circuits around the sacred fire, garments tied, while mantras are chanted. Most North Indian traditions take seven circuits; Gujarati weddings take four, standing for dharma, artha, kama and moksha, the four aims of life. Who leads which circuits, and whether the bride's brother p"},{"name":"Saptapadi, the seven steps","description":"The couple take seven steps together before the fire, each step a stated vow: nourishment, strength, prosperity, happiness, children, the seasons of life, and lifelong friendship, with wording varying by tradition. In several communities this, not the pheras, is the decisive act; Indian law agrees, "},{"name":"Mangalsutra and thali","description":"The groom fastens the marriage necklace on the bride at the ritual high point: black beads and gold in most of India, guarding the marriage against ill fortune; in Tamil and Telugu weddings the thali or mangalyam is tied with three knots to a crescendo of drumming, with the third knot often tied by "},{"name":"Sindoor","description":"The groom applies vermilion powder into the parting of the bride's hair, the visible mark of a married woman in much of India. In Bengali weddings the sindoor daan is the emotional peak of the ceremony, with the bride's face traditionally shielded by a new sari held over her head as it is applied."},{"name":"Regional ceremony forms","description":"The core is shared; the staging differs. Tamil weddings hold the muhurtham in the morning with the oonjal swing ceremony and, in Brahmin families, the kashi yatra, where the groom feigns leaving for a life of study until the bride's father wins him back. Telugu weddings paste cumin and jaggery (jeel"},{"name":"Haldi","description":"Turmeric paste applied to the couple by relatives in turn, for glow, blessing and protection before the wedding. Known as pithi in Gujarati families, gaye holud in Bengali ones where the turmeric touched to the groom is carried to the bride, and paired in the south with the mangala snanam purifying "},{"name":"Mehndi","description":"The bride's hands and feet are covered in henna, with the groom's name or initials hidden in the design. Folklore says the darker the stain, the deeper the love, or the better the mother-in-law. The lawn of aunties having their own hands done is as much the point as the bridal application."},{"name":"Sangeet","description":"The night of performances: choreographed dances by cousins and friends, songs teasing both families, and open dancing after. It grew from the North Indian ladies' sangeet of folk songs at home into the biggest production of many modern weddings, and it is where the two families genuinely mix before "},{"name":"Joota chupai","description":"The bride's sisters and cousins steal the groom's shoes when he removes them to enter the mandap, and ransom them back with theatrical negotiation as the ceremony ends. A beloved North Indian and Punjabi game; the groom's side is honour-bound to attempt rescue, and the price is agreed with much perf"},{"name":"Vidaai","description":"The bride's farewell to her family, leaving as part of a new household. In many North Indian families she throws handfuls of rice back over her head towards her parents' home in thanks for all they gave her, and there is rarely a dry eye. Modern couples who go home together that night still often ke"},{"name":"Griha pravesh","description":"The bride's welcome into the groom's family home: an aarti at the door, then she tips a vessel of rice over the threshold with her right foot, abundance entering with her, and steps in right foot first. In some families she presses her hands in kumkum or steps in a red solution to leave footprints o"},{"name":"Aashirvad, the blessings of elders","description":"Throughout the wedding the couple touch the feet of parents, grandparents and elder relatives, who bless them in return. It punctuates every event, and elders expect it; a couple who move through the room touching feet after the ceremony are doing the rounds every Hindu wedding requires."},{"name":"Gold as blessing","description":"Gold given at a wedding is blessing, security and inheritance at once, and bridal gold is traditionally regarded as the bride's own property, her stridhan. Families often buy pieces over years for a child's wedding, and time purchases to auspicious days. The weight of gold at South Indian weddings i"},{"name":"The vegetarian feast","description":"Food at the ceremony is normally fully vegetarian, cooked without beef ever and often without eggs, onion or garlic depending on the community, and many families keep the entire wedding vegetarian. This is a religious matter of purity around a sacred rite, not a menu preference, and guests broadly e"}],"gotchas":["Set the muhurat before you book anything. The auspicious time can land at dawn, late evening or in the middle of the night, and the ceremony's key moment must hit it, so venue access hours, the hair and make-up start time and the whole run sheet hang off a time you do not control","Muhurat dates cluster. The panchang gives everyone the same auspicious dates, so in India venues, pandits, photographers and even flight prices spike on them, and diaspora vendors serving the community feel the same crunch. Book earlier than a Western wedding of the same size wou","Whole stretches of the calendar have no wedding dates at all: Chaturmas in many communities, adhik maas, and periods when Venus or Jupiter is combust. If your dream month falls in one, no pandit will move it, so check the year's calendar with your pandit before falling in love wi","The havan means a real fire indoors. Many Western venues refuse open flames or handle them badly; you need written permission, a fire-safe mandap setup, and smoke detectors that will not empty the building mid-ceremony. Experienced mandap companies manage this with the venue, whi","A Hindu ceremony in England and Wales usually has no legal effect on its own, because most hotels and marquees are not registered for marriage. Couples who skip the civil step are legally unmarried, which surfaces years later in property, inheritance and visa problems. Decide the","In India, registration alone does not make a marriage: courts have held that a registered marriage without the actual ceremonies is not valid, and equally that the certificate, not the wedding album, is what officials accept as proof. You need both the rites and the registration.","The ceremony runs ninety minutes to three hours and guests treat it accordingly: coming and going, chatting at the back, watching the key moments. Guests from other backgrounds will not know this is normal. A printed program, or a pandit or emcee who explains as it goes, transfor","Vegetarian catering is a competence, not a menu toggle. A venue's in-house caterer who 'can do Indian' is the most common catering disappointment; most families bring in an Indian caterer or choose venues that allow one. Check whether eggs count as vegetarian for your families, a","If any event is at a temple, the premises rules are absolute: vegetarian only, no alcohol, often including the car park, and modest dress. Do not plan a champagne toast anywhere near the temple hall.","Alcohol expectations differ between families more than almost anything else. One side may assume an open bar at the sangeet, the other may be dry on religious grounds. Settle it between the parents early, event by event, before contracts are signed.","The baraat always runs late. Dhol players, dancing relatives and a horse do not move to schedule, so build thirty to sixty minutes of slack between the baraat's start and the muhurat, and tell the photographer where it will pause. Street processions and horses may also need counc","Guest counts behave differently: parents hold real invitation power, invitations go to households not individuals, and the reception list often exceeds the ceremony list. Expect the list to grow after you 'finalise' it, and cater with a buffer rather than an exact count.","Outfit lead times are long. Made-to-order lehengas and sherwanis from India need months, plus shipping, customs and alteration rounds, and a buying trip to India is itself a scheduling event. Order early and plan fittings close to the wedding, since heavy outfits need final adjus","Bridal mehndi takes three to eight hours and the stain needs a day to deepen, which locks the bride's diary a day or two before the wedding. Nothing else that needs her hands, including final errands, can share that day.","Gold prices move. Families budgeting jewellery as one late purchase get caught by a rising market; buying over months spreads the risk. Insure the jewellery for the events themselves, when large amounts are worn, carried and set down in busy venues.","Traditionally the bride's family hosted the wedding and the groom's the reception, but modern couples split costs every possible way. The split is the single most common source of inter-family friction, so agree it explicitly, event by event, at the start.","Pandits get booked like venues, on the same clustered dates. In the diaspora especially, book the pandit when you book the venue, confirm the language mix, whether he will explain rituals in English, and exactly which rituals your ceremony will include, since a full ceremony and ","The decisive ritual moments, the mangalsutra, the sindoor, the seventh step, each last seconds and happen low to the ground surrounded by seated family. A photographer who has never shot a Hindu wedding will miss them. Ask to see full Hindu wedding galleries, not portfolio highli"]},"jewish":{"id":"jewish","name":"Jewish","shortName":"Jewish","faithGroup":"Jewish","blurb":"Signing the ketubah, the bedeken, standing together under the chuppah for the sheva brachot and the breaking of the glass, then the hora — with the aufruf before and, in traditional communities, a week of festive meals after.","typicalGuests":{"low":80,"typical":180,"high":500},"terms":{"officiant":"rabbi","ceremonyVenue":"chuppah venue","ceremony":"the chuppah","bestMan":"best man","maidOfHonour":"maid of honour","reception":"reception","firstDance":"hora","witnesses":"eidim"},"events":[{"id":"engagement","name":"Engagement party (vort or tenaim)","when":"Six to eighteen months before","optional":true,"default":false,"description":"In many Orthodox communities the engagement is marked with a vort, a gathering with short speeches, or a tenaim ceremony where the two families formally agree to the match and the mothers break a plate. Many families now sign the tenaim on "},{"id":"henna","name":"Henna night","when":"In the week before","optional":true,"default":false,"description":"A pre-wedding celebration from Sephardi and Mizrahi communities, especially those with roots in Morocco, Yemen, Iraq, Iran and elsewhere in the Middle East and North Africa. Henna paste is applied to the hands of the couple and guests, the "},{"id":"aufruf","name":"Aufruf and Shabbat kallah","when":"The Shabbat before, or the one before that","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"The groom, or in egalitarian communities the couple together, is called up to the Torah in synagogue and the congregation sings and throws soft sweets. The family usually sponsors the kiddush afterwards. In communities where the couple do n"},{"id":"mikveh","name":"Mikveh immersion","when":"In the days before","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"The bride immerses in the mikveh, the ritual bath, in the days before the wedding, with a preparation appointment and an attendant present. In traditional practice this is required rather than optional, and the wedding date is often set wit"},{"id":"civil-ceremony","name":"Civil ceremony or registration","when":"Before or on the wedding day, depending on country","optional":true,"default":false,"description":"Whether you need a separate civil step depends on the country. In England and Wales the religious ceremony can be legally binding after civil preliminaries, with the synagogue's secretary for marriages registering it. In the United States, "},{"id":"tisch","name":"Kabbalat panim and the tisch","when":"Wedding day, before the ceremony","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"Two receptions before the ceremony. At the kabbalat panim the bride is greeted and blessed by guests; at the groom's tisch there is singing, a word of Torah that friends traditionally interrupt, and the signing of the ketubah in front of th"},{"id":"ceremony","name":"The chuppah","when":"Wedding day","optional":false,"default":true,"description":"The ceremony under the wedding canopy joins what were historically two separate ceremonies. Kiddushin, the betrothal: a blessing over wine and the giving of the ring with the words 'Harei at mekudeshet li' before two witnesses. Then the ket"},{"id":"yichud","name":"Yichud","when":"Straight after the ceremony","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"The couple withdraw to a private room for a short time alone, with the door watched by two witnesses in Orthodox practice. If they have been fasting they break the fast together here. It is a required part of the marriage in Ashkenazi Ortho"},{"id":"reception","name":"The reception (seudat mitzvah)","when":"Wedding day","optional":false,"default":true,"description":"The wedding meal is a seudat mitzvah, a religious celebration in its own right, and gladdening the couple is considered a mitzvah for every guest. Expect the hora early rather than late, with the couple lifted on chairs, and at Orthodox wed"},{"id":"sheva-brachot-week","name":"The week of sheva brachot","when":"The seven days after","optional":true,"default":false,"description":"In traditional communities, friends and family host festive meals for the couple on each of the seven nights after the wedding, ending with the seven blessings again. Each meal needs a minyan and, apart from Shabbat, at least one guest who "}],"items":[{"id":"rabbi-fee","name":"Rabbi's fee","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"The officiant's fee or honorarium, usually covering the preparation meetings as well as the day. If your rabbi is travelling to the venue, cover travel, and accommodation if the wedding runs close to ","essential":true,"share":0.015,"supplier":"Synagogue","religious":true},{"id":"cantor-fee","name":"Cantor (chazan)","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"Many couples have a cantor sing the blessings under the chuppah, particularly the sheva brachot. Optional if your rabbi sings, or if you are giving the blessings to friends and relatives as honours.","essential":false,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Synagogue","religious":false},{"id":"synagogue-fee","name":"Synagogue or ceremony venue fee","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"A chuppah can stand anywhere — synagogue, hotel, garden or hall — so this is the fee for wherever the ceremony happens. Synagogues usually charge less for members, and in the United Kingdom the offici","essential":true,"share":0.012,"supplier":"Synagogue","religious":false},{"id":"marriage-authorisation","name":"Religious marriage authorisation fees","section":"Ceremony","event":"","note":"Orthodox marriages generally need formal authorisation before the wedding: in the United Kingdom from the Office of the Chief Rabbi (or the equivalent body for your community), in Israel by opening a ","essential":true,"share":0.003,"supplier":"Synagogue","religious":true},{"id":"civil-fees","name":"Civil notice and registration fees","section":"Ceremony","event":"civil-ceremony","note":"The state's fees: notice of marriage at the register office in England and Wales, the marriage licence in the United States and Canada, the Notice of Intended Marriage in Australia, the town-hall cere","essential":true,"share":0.003,"supplier":"Registrar","religious":false},{"id":"ketubah","name":"Ketubah","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"The marriage document itself, signed before the ceremony and read under the chuppah, is often commissioned as artwork to hang at home for life. Prices run from a printed text to hand-painted calligrap","essential":true,"share":0.007,"supplier":"Judaica","religious":true},{"id":"ceremony-wine-glass","name":"Kiddush cups, wine and the glass to break","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"Two cups of kosher wine are blessed under the chuppah, one for the betrothal and one for the seven blessings. Families often lend heirloom kiddush cups. You also need the glass to break, wrapped in cl","essential":true,"share":0.002,"supplier":"Judaica","religious":true},{"id":"kippot","name":"Kippot for guests","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"Personalised kippot (skullcaps) printed with your names and the date, offered in a basket for guests to wear. Order per guest with plenty spare; they double as a favour. Less expected at secular cerem","essential":false,"share":0.004,"supplier":"Judaica","religious":false},{"id":"mikveh-fee","name":"Mikveh fees","section":"Ceremony","event":"mikveh","note":"The immersion appointment, usually with a preparation room and attendant. Some communities also expect the groom to immerse, often at a men's session. A modest fee, but book it early because the timin","essential":false,"share":0.001,"supplier":"Mikveh","religious":false},{"id":"aufruf-kiddush","name":"Aufruf kiddush","section":"Ceremony","event":"aufruf","note":"Sponsoring the kiddush, the refreshments after the Shabbat morning service, when the groom or couple is called to the Torah. Ranges from wine and cake to a full lunch. The soft sweets thrown in the sy","essential":false,"share":0.006,"supplier":"Synagogue","religious":false},{"id":"chuppah-hire","name":"Chuppah hire or build","section":"Flowers & Styling","event":"ceremony","note":"The canopy itself: four poles and a covering, open on all four sides. Florists and venues hire structures from simple to elaborate, but a tallit (prayer shawl) held on four poles by friends is complet","essential":true,"share":0.014,"supplier":"Florist","religious":true},{"id":"chuppah-flowers","name":"Chuppah and ceremony flowers","section":"Flowers & Styling","event":"ceremony","note":"Dressing the chuppah is the main floral moment of the ceremony, and a flower-covered chuppah is one of the bigger single floral items on the market. Aisle and entrance arrangements are the same as at ","essential":true,"share":0.018,"supplier":"Florist","religious":false},{"id":"reception-styling","name":"Table centrepieces and reception styling","section":"Flowers & Styling","event":"reception","note":"Centrepieces, linen and room styling, priced per table. If there will be energetic circle dancing, keep the dance floor clear of anything that can be knocked over and ask the florist to weight tall ar","essential":true,"share":0.03,"supplier":"Florist","religious":false},{"id":"bouquets","name":"Bouquets and buttonholes","section":"Flowers & Styling","event":"ceremony","note":"Bouquet for the bride, buttonholes for the groom, fathers and grandfathers, corsages for the mothers. Standard wedding florals with no particular religious rules attached.","essential":true,"share":0.007,"supplier":"Florist","religious":false},{"id":"venue-hire","name":"Reception venue hire","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"If you need fully kosher catering, the venue question comes first: some venues have kosher kitchens or are on a community's approved list, others must be koshered for the day or allow an outside koshe","essential":true,"share":0.105,"supplier":"Venue","religious":false},{"id":"catering","name":"Catering","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"The largest line. Fully supervised kosher catering costs noticeably more per head than standard catering: a restricted supplier pool, supervision, and koshering or bringing in equipment all add cost. ","essential":true,"share":0.26,"supplier":"Caterer","religious":false},{"id":"kashrut-supervision","name":"Kashrut supervision (mashgiach)","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"Fully kosher events need a mashgiach, the supervisor licensed by a kashrut authority who oversees the kitchen on the day. Sometimes included in the caterer's price, sometimes billed separately, plus t","essential":false,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Caterer","religious":false},{"id":"drinks","name":"Drinks and kosher wine","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"At a supervised event all wine and grape products must be kosher, and many authorities expect mevushal wine when waiting staff are not observant Jews, so agree the wine list with the caterer or superv","essential":true,"share":0.06,"supplier":"Caterer","religious":false},{"id":"cake","name":"Wedding cake and desserts","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"After a kosher meat meal the cake must be parev — made without dairy — which rules out buttercream and many standard recipes, so use a kosher baker or confirm your baker can work parev under supervisi","essential":true,"share":0.015,"supplier":"Cake maker","religious":false},{"id":"service-staffing","name":"Service charge and staffing","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"Waiting and bar staff, corkage and cake-cutting fees. Kosher caterers working in a non-kosher venue often bring their own equipment and extra staff, which shows up here.","essential":true,"share":0.012,"supplier":"Caterer","religious":false},{"id":"sheva-brachot-hosting","name":"Sheva brachot meals contribution","section":"Reception","event":"sheva-brachot-week","note":"The week of festive meals is traditionally hosted and paid for by friends and family rather than the couple, but many couples contribute to the bigger nights or host the final one. Only relevant if yo","essential":false,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Caterer","religious":false},{"id":"band","name":"Live band","section":"Music & Entertainment","event":"reception","note":"The band makes or breaks the hora, so book one that knows Jewish weddings: the standard simcha repertoire, when to strike up as you enter from yichud, and how to keep a twenty-minute dance set going. ","essential":true,"share":0.055,"supplier":"Band","religious":false},{"id":"dj","name":"DJ","section":"Music & Entertainment","event":"reception","note":"Instead of or alongside the band, often taking the later part of the night. Give them the same briefing on the hora set and any Hebrew or Israeli must-plays.","essential":false,"share":0.016,"supplier":"DJ","religious":false},{"id":"ceremony-music","name":"Ceremony musicians","section":"Music & Entertainment","event":"ceremony","note":"Music for the processional and under the chuppah — a string ensemble, a singer, or the band's acoustic players. Traditional processional pieces exist but there is no fixed liturgy for the walk in, so ","essential":false,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Band","religious":false},{"id":"shtick","name":"Shtick and dancing props","section":"Music & Entertainment","event":"reception","note":"At traditional weddings guests entertain the couple during the dancing — juggling, signs, masks, jump ropes, costumes — known as shtick. Families often buy a box of props in advance. Inexpensive and h","essential":false,"share":0.003,"supplier":"Entertainment","religious":false},{"id":"photographer","name":"Photographer","section":"Photos & Film","event":"","note":"Book someone who knows the running order: the ketubah signing, bedeken, glass and hora are fast, one-off moments. If you are not seeing each other before the bedeken there is no 'first look', so portr","essential":true,"share":0.055,"supplier":"Photographer","religious":false},{"id":"videographer","name":"Videographer","section":"Photos & Film","event":"","note":"The moments couples most regret losing are the bedeken, the sheva brachot being sung, the glass and the first hora set. Brief them on where the witnesses and rabbi will stand so the ring moment is not","essential":false,"share":0.035,"supplier":"Videographer","religious":false},{"id":"album","name":"Album and prints","section":"Photos & Film","event":"","note":"Usually ordered after the wedding, with copies for both sets of parents. The signed ketubah photographed well is a favourite opening page.","essential":false,"share":0.007,"supplier":"Photographer","religious":false},{"id":"gown","name":"Wedding dress","section":"Attire","event":"","note":"In Orthodox settings dresses are modest by convention — shoulders and often collarbone covered, sometimes with a detachable topper for the ceremony that comes off for dancing. Elsewhere anything goes.","essential":true,"share":0.055,"supplier":"Bridal shop","religious":false},{"id":"bridal-accessories","name":"Veil, shoes and accessories","section":"Attire","event":"","note":"The veil matters more than usual because of the bedeken, where the groom lowers it over the bride's face before the ceremony — choose one that can actually be lowered and lifted. Comfortable shoes or ","essential":true,"share":0.01,"supplier":"Bridal shop","religious":false},{"id":"groom-outfit","name":"Groom's outfit and kittel","section":"Attire","event":"","note":"Suit or, in more formal communities, a dark suit or frock coat. Many Ashkenazi grooms wear a kittel, a simple white robe, over the suit under the chuppah — often borrowed or a family one rather than b","essential":true,"share":0.018,"supplier":"Menswear","religious":false},{"id":"party-attire","name":"Wedding party outfits","section":"Attire","event":"","note":"Bridesmaids and groomsmen are a modern addition rather than a Jewish requirement; the traditional escorts are the parents, who walk each of you down the aisle. Whether the couple pays for outfits foll","essential":false,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Bridal shop","religious":false},{"id":"hair-makeup","name":"Hair and make-up","section":"Beauty","event":"","note":"Timed around the kabbalat panim rather than the ceremony, since you are receiving guests and being photographed an hour or more before the chuppah. Book the trial well ahead. If the bride is immersing","essential":true,"share":0.016,"supplier":"Hair and make-up","religious":false},{"id":"invitations","name":"Invitations","section":"Stationery","event":"","note":"Traditionally printed with the Hebrew date alongside the civil one, and often with Hebrew text on one side or one panel. Both sets of parents are conventionally named as hosts. Check the Hebrew spelli","essential":true,"share":0.015,"supplier":"Stationer","religious":false},{"id":"benchers-programs","name":"Benchers and order of service","section":"Stationery","event":"reception","note":"Benchers (birkonim) are booklets with Grace after Meals and the sheva brachot, printed with your names and date, one per guest or per couple, taken home as a keepsake. An order-of-service sheet explai","essential":false,"share":0.005,"supplier":"Stationer","religious":false},{"id":"favours","name":"Favours","section":"Stationery","event":"reception","note":"No fixed Jewish tradition here — kippot and benchers often serve as the favours. Some couples give a donation to charity in guests' names instead, in the spirit of tzedakah.","essential":false,"share":0.005,"supplier":"Stationer","religious":false},{"id":"wedding-cars","name":"Wedding cars","section":"Transport","event":"ceremony","note":"Standard wedding transport. If any of the wedding events touch Shabbat — an aufruf lunch, a Saturday night ceremony — observant guests will not drive until Shabbat ends, which affects timings more tha","essential":false,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Car hire","religious":false},{"id":"guest-transport","name":"Guest transport","section":"Transport","event":"reception","note":"Coaches are worth it when the ceremony and reception are apart, or for a Saturday night wedding ending very late. For guests walking to a Shabbat-adjacent event, check the venue is within walking dist","essential":false,"share":0.006,"supplier":"Coach hire","religious":false},{"id":"rings","name":"Wedding rings","section":"Rings & Gifts","event":"ceremony","note":"The ring given under the chuppah is traditionally a plain metal band with no stones, owned outright by the giver, placed on the right index finger before the witnesses. Engagement rings with stones ar","essential":true,"share":0.025,"supplier":"Jeweller","religious":true},{"id":"gifts","name":"Gifts for parents, honourees and each other","section":"Rings & Gifts","event":"","note":"Small gifts for those given honours — the sheva brachot reciters, the chuppah holders, the witnesses — plus parents and each other. Judaica such as a kiddush cup or candlesticks is the conventional ch","essential":false,"share":0.006,"supplier":"Gifts","religious":false},{"id":"planner","name":"Wedding planner or coordinator","section":"Extras","event":"","note":"Useful for any large wedding, and particularly for kosher logistics in a non-kosher venue, Saturday night turnarounds, or an Israel wedding planned from abroad. Choose someone who has actually run the","essential":false,"share":0.025,"supplier":"Planner","religious":false},{"id":"henna-party","name":"Henna night","section":"Extras","event":"henna","note":"For Sephardi and Mizrahi families: the henna artist, traditional costumes (often hired), sweets, music and a venue or a family home. Scales from an evening at home to a second full event, especially i","essential":false,"share":0.01,"supplier":"Entertainment","religious":false},{"id":"security","name":"Security","section":"Extras","event":"","note":"Many Jewish communal venues, especially in Europe, use trained security at large events as standard, sometimes arranged through communal security bodies. Some synagogues include it in the venue fee; p","essential":false,"share":0.006,"supplier":"Security","religious":false},{"id":"tzedakah","name":"Tzedakah (charitable giving)","section":"Extras","event":"","note":"Giving to charity in connection with the wedding is a widespread custom across all streams — a donation on the day, a percentage of the budget, or leftover food to a food charity. Entirely voluntary, ","essential":false,"share":0.004,"supplier":"Charity","religious":false}],"timeline":[{"mb":12,"task":"Check dates against the Jewish calendar before booking anything: no weddings on Shabbat or festivals, and most of the Omer and the Three Weeks are closed in traditional practice — ask your rabbi exactly which days your community permits","critical":true},{"mb":12,"task":"Choose your officiant and stream early — Orthodox, Conservative/Masorti, Reform/Liberal or a secular celebrant — because it determines the paperwork, the ketubah text, the venue options and, for mixed-faith couples, who will marry you at all","critical":true},{"mb":12,"task":"Book the rabbi and the ceremony venue together, and confirm the date is in the synagogue diary","critical":true},{"mb":11,"task":"If either of you was previously married in a Jewish ceremony, start the get (religious divorce) now — Orthodox and Conservative rabbis cannot marry you without it, and it takes months","critical":true},{"mb":11,"task":"Gather proof of Jewish status for an Orthodox marriage: parents' ketubah, a letter from a recognised rabbi, or conversion certificates — records held abroad can take a long time to obtain","critical":true},{"mb":11,"task":"Book the reception venue, checking kosher catering arrangements, dance floor size for the hora, and a private room for yichud","critical":true},{"mb":10,"task":"Book the kosher caterer, or agree with the venue how the kitchen will be supervised or koshered — the approved-caterer list for your kashrut authority narrows the field, so move early","critical":true},{"mb":10,"task":"Book the band, photographer and videographer, briefing them on the tisch, bedeken, chuppah and hora running order","critical":false},{"mb":9,"task":"Apply for religious marriage authorisation: through the Office of the Chief Rabbi in the United Kingdom, your movement's equivalent elsewhere, or by planning the Rabbinate marriage file if marrying in Israel","critical":true},{"mb":8,"task":"Order the ketubah, agreeing the exact text with your rabbi before the artist starts — commissioned pieces take weeks to months","critical":true},{"mb":7,"task":"Arrange the chuppah: hire one, ask the synagogue about theirs, or plan to use a family tallit on poles held by friends","critical":false},{"mb":7,"task":"Begin marriage preparation with your rabbi — most movements expect a few meetings, and Orthodox communities run chatan and kallah classes","critical":true},{"mb":6,"task":"Choose the two witnesses (eidim) with your rabbi's guidance — in Orthodox practice they must be religiously observant Jewish men unrelated to either of you or to each other, so do not assume close family can do it","critical":true},{"mb":6,"task":"Order invitations with the Hebrew date, and confirm the Hebrew spelling of both your names and your parents' Hebrew names — the same details go on the ketubah","critical":false},{"mb":5,"task":"Decide who receives honours: sheva brachot reciters, chuppah pole holders, ketubah reader — traditional weddings have many honours to give, which is a gift to both families","critical":false},{"mb":4,"task":"Order kippot and benchers printed with your names and the date","critical":false},{"mb":4,"task":"Buy the wedding band — a plain metal ring with no stones for the ceremony, owned by the giver — and a second one if you are doing a double-ring exchange","critical":false},{"mb":3,"task":"Complete civil preliminaries: give notice at the register office in England and Wales, lodge the Notice of Intended Marriage in Australia, or diarise the marriage licence window in the United States and Canada","critical":true},{"mb":3,"task":"Arrange the aufruf with your synagogue and book the kiddush","critical":false},{"mb":2,"task":"Book the mikveh appointment for the days before the wedding, and a men's session for the groom if he is immersing","critical":false},{"mb":2,"task":"Finalise the menu with the caterer and kashrut supervisor, remembering a meat meal means a parev dessert and cake","critical":false},{"mb":2,"task":"If keeping the week of sheva brachot, line up hosts for each night and warn them about the panim chadashot — each meal needs a guest who was not at the wedding","critical":false},{"mb":1,"task":"Walk through the ceremony with your rabbi: the ketubah signing, the bedeken, who walks with whom, circling, the ring declaration and the glass","critical":true},{"mb":1,"task":"Confirm timings for a Shabbat-adjacent date: a Saturday night wedding cannot start until Shabbat ends, which in summer is very late — check havdalah time, supplier overtime and venue closing","critical":true},{"mb":1,"task":"Arrange the yichud room with the venue, including food and drink to break the wedding-day fast if you are fasting","critical":false},{"mb":0,"task":"Bring the ketubah, the ring, the wrapped glass, the kiddush cups and wine, and the kittel if wearing one — give one person the job of having them all at the venue","critical":true},{"mb":0,"task":"After the wedding, make sure the marriage is civilly registered: the synagogue's secretary for marriages files it in England and Wales, the officiant returns the licence in North America, and the Rabbinate issues the certificate in Israel — confirm it has actually been done","critical":true}],"paperwork":[{"item":"Proof of Jewish status","who":"Both, for Orthodox and Conservative marriages","note":"Typically your parents' ketubah, your own or a parent's synagogue marriage record, or a letter from a recognised rabbi. Orthodox authorities follow matrilineal descent or an Orthodox conversion; Conservative similarly. R"},{"item":"Religious marriage authorisation","who":"Both","note":"In the United Kingdom, Orthodox marriages require authorisation from the Office of the Chief Rabbi (or the relevant authority for Sephardi, Federation, Masorti, Reform or Liberal communities), with a document meeting and"},{"item":"Civil marriage licence or notice","who":"Both","note":"Varies entirely by country. England and Wales: notice at the register office at least 29 days before (longer for some nationalities). United States and Canada: a state or provincial licence, signed by the rabbi. Australi"},{"item":"Ketubah","who":"Both","note":"Signed by the two witnesses just before the ceremony, read under the chuppah, and kept by the couple — traditionally in the wife's keeping, since it records obligations towards her. The text must match your officiant's r"},{"item":"Get (religious divorce) from any previous Jewish marriage","who":"Either party previously married","note":"A civil divorce is not sufficient for Orthodox or Conservative remarriage; a get from a recognised beit din is required and can take months. British Reform operates its own beit din and also requires a get; American Refo"},{"item":"Conversion certificate","who":"Either party who converted","note":"Recognition varies sharply by movement: Orthodox authorities accept only conversions under recognised Orthodox auspices, while Conservative, Reform and Liberal communities accept their own and often each other's. If you "},{"item":"Civil divorce decree or death certificate of a former spouse","who":"Either party previously married or widowed","note":"The final decree, not the provisional one, for the civil authorities — needed alongside, not instead of, the get where a get applies."},{"item":"Birth certificates and passports","who":"Both","note":"For the civil preliminaries, and for the Rabbinate in Israel, which may also ask for parents' documents when checking status."},{"item":"Tenaim (engagement contract)","who":"Both, optional","note":"A traditional document formalising the engagement, still used in many Orthodox communities and often signed at the wedding itself, marked by the mothers breaking a plate. Not a legal requirement anywhere; most non-Orthod"},{"item":"Marriage schedule and registration (England and Wales)","who":"Both","note":"Jewish marriages have a distinct legal route under the Marriage Act: after civil notice, the marriage can be solemnised 'according to the usages of the Jews' at any location, and the synagogue's appointed secretary for m"},{"item":"Rabbinate marriage file (Israel)","who":"Both, if marrying in Israel","note":"There is no civil marriage in Israel: a legally recognised Jewish wedding must go through the Chief Rabbinate, which requires proof of Jewish status to Orthodox standards, proof you are unmarried, and details of the kosh"},{"item":"Officiant's civil authority to solemnise","who":"The officiant","note":"In the United States, Canada and Australia the rabbi normally acts for the state too, but registration rules vary by state, province and even city, so confirm your rabbi is authorised where you are marrying — especially "},{"item":"Kashrut certification for the reception","who":"The couple, via caterer and venue","note":"Where the wedding is under Orthodox auspices, the reception is usually expected to be supervised by a recognised kashrut authority — the United Synagogue requires this in Britain, and the Israeli Rabbinate asks about the"}],"customs":[{"name":"The ketubah","description":"The Jewish marriage document, some two thousand years old as a form, traditionally written in Aramaic and recording the husband's obligations to his wife. It is signed by two witnesses before the ceremony and read aloud under the chuppah. Conservative ketubot often add the Lieberman clause, agreeing"},{"name":"The chuppah","description":"The four-posted canopy, open on all sides, under which the ceremony happens. It stands for the home the couple will build: open like Abraham and Sarah's tent, sheltered above. It can be a florist's structure or a family tallit on four poles held by friends, and Ashkenazi custom favours placing it un"},{"name":"Kiddushin and nisuin: the two ceremonies","description":"A Jewish wedding is historically two ceremonies. Kiddushin (also called erusin), the betrothal, is effected when the ring is given and accepted before witnesses; nisuin, the marriage proper, is the seven blessings and the couple's first moments together. For around a thousand years the two have been"},{"name":"The ring and the declaration","description":"The ring is a plain band without stones, owned by the giver, placed on the recipient's right index finger with the declaration 'Harei at mekudeshet li b'taba'at zo k'dat Moshe v'Yisrael' — 'By this ring you are consecrated to me according to the law of Moses and Israel' — in front of the two witness"},{"name":"The bedeken","description":"Before the ceremony the groom, accompanied by singing and dancing, lowers the veil over the bride's face. The custom recalls Rebecca veiling herself on meeting Isaac, and is often explained as the groom confirming he is marrying the right person — unlike Jacob, who wasn't. Parents commonly bless the"},{"name":"Kabbalat panim and the tisch","description":"The pre-ceremony receptions: the bride enthroned among her guests at the kabbalat panim, the groom at his tisch with song, drink and an attempted Torah discourse that friends traditionally drown out. The ketubah is signed here. Many egalitarian couples hold one combined tisch or each hold their own "},{"name":"Circling","description":"In Ashkenazi custom the bride circles the groom, traditionally seven times, on arriving under the chuppah — variously explained as building the walls of their new home or a protective circle. Many contemporary couples across all streams circle each other, three times each and one together, or omit i"},{"name":"The sheva brachot","description":"The seven blessings sung over wine that form the heart of nisuin, moving from the creation of the world to the joy of this particular couple, ending with the voice of joy and gladness, the voice of the groom and the voice of the bride. Distributing them among honoured relatives, friends and teachers"},{"name":"Breaking the glass","description":"At the end of the ceremony a glass, wrapped for safety, is broken underfoot and everyone calls 'Mazal tov!'. The classic explanation is remembering the destruction of the Temple in Jerusalem at the height of joy; others hear it as a reminder of the world's brokenness or of love's fragility. Traditio"},{"name":"Yichud","description":"Straight after the chuppah the couple spend a short time completely alone in a private room — in Orthodox practice a formal part of the marriage, with witnesses at the door. It is where a fasting couple break their fast, and for everyone it is a rare quiet moment on a loud day. Standard in Ashkenazi"},{"name":"The hora and gladdening the couple","description":"The circle dance to 'Hava Nagila' and the simcha repertoire, with the couple lifted on chairs at the centre, often holding a napkin or handkerchief between them. Making the couple rejoice is counted a mitzvah, which is why guests perform, juggle and hold up signs (shtick) rather than just dancing. A"},{"name":"The aufruf","description":"On a Shabbat before the wedding the groom — or the couple together in egalitarian communities — is called up to the Torah, and the congregation sings and throws soft sweets for a sweet life. A kiddush follows. In Sephardi custom the celebratory aliyah traditionally comes on the Shabbat after the wed"},{"name":"Mikveh before the wedding","description":"The bride immerses in the ritual bath in the days before the wedding — in traditional practice a requirement, timed carefully, and treated as a private and moving threshold moment. Many grooms immerse too as a custom. Non-Orthodox movements have warmly reclaimed pre-wedding immersion as spiritual pr"},{"name":"Fasting on the wedding day","description":"Ashkenazi custom treats the wedding day as a personal Yom Kippur: the couple fast from dawn until they break the fast together after the ceremony, and the groom's tisch and the quiet of the day carry some of that solemnity. Sephardi practice generally does not include fasting, and the fast is waived"},{"name":"The kittel","description":"Many Ashkenazi grooms wear a kittel, the simple white robe also worn on Yom Kippur, over their suit under the chuppah — white for purity and a clean slate, and a quiet reminder of mortality at the moment of greatest joy. The bride's white dress is often given the same explanation."},{"name":"Not seeing each other beforehand","description":"In traditional Ashkenazi practice the couple do not see each other for the week before the wedding, meeting again only at the bedeken, which gives that moment its charge. Many couples keep just the wedding day itself, or only the hour before; others skip the custom entirely and do a first look for p"},{"name":"The week of sheva brachot","description":"For seven days after the wedding, traditional communities feast the couple: a festive meal each evening hosted by different friends or relatives, ending with the seven blessings sung again over wine. Each gathering needs a minyan and, except on Shabbat, panim chadashot — a new face, someone who was "},{"name":"Henna night","description":"Sephardi and Mizrahi families, particularly of Moroccan, Yemenite, Iraqi and Persian heritage, hold a henna celebration in the week before the wedding: henna paste pressed into the palms of the couple and guests for blessing and protection, traditional costume, songs and tables of sweets. It has had"},{"name":"Breaking a plate at the tenaim","description":"When the tenaim, the engagement conditions, are signed — historically at the engagement, now often on the wedding day — the two mothers break a china plate together. Like the glass at the ceremony's end, an unrepairable break marks an unrepealable commitment. Kept mainly in Orthodox and traditional "},{"name":"The mezinke","description":"An Ashkenazi folk custom from the klezmer tradition: when parents marry off their last child, they are seated in the middle of the dance floor, crowned with flowers, and the guests dance around them to the tune 'Di Mezinke Oysgegebn'. A lovely thing to warn your band about in advance if it applies."},{"name":"Auspicious and avoided days","description":"Tuesday is traditionally considered a lucky day to marry, because in the creation story God says 'it was good' twice on the third day. Many communities also favour Rosh Chodesh, the new month. These are customs rather than law — unlike Shabbat, festivals and the mourning seasons, which are genuine c"},{"name":"Egalitarian and same-sex adaptations","description":"Reform, Liberal, Reconstructionist and, since 2012, Conservative movements offer marriage ceremonies for same-sex couples, adapting the ketubah, the ring declarations and rituals like the bedeken and circling. Many opposite-sex couples in these movements use the same egalitarian forms — double rings"}],"gotchas":["Check the Jewish calendar before you fall in love with a date. Shabbat (Friday sundown to Saturday nightfall), festivals and their eves are closed in all traditional practice, and two mourning seasons — roughly a month of the Omer in spring and the Three Weeks in high summer — cl","Omer observance varies widely. Traditional practice keeps around 33 days of the period between Pesach and Shavuot wedding-free, but which days are open — Lag BaOmer, Rosh Chodesh, before or after — differs by community, Ashkenazi and Sephardi custom, and movement. Conservative ru","The Three Weeks (17 Tammuz to Tisha B'Av, usually July into August) fall exactly in peak wedding season. Ashkenazi Orthodox practice holds no weddings for the whole period; Sephardi custom is often stricter only from the start of Av; Conservative authorities permit ceremonies unt","A Saturday night wedding cannot start until Shabbat ends at nightfall, which in northern-hemisphere summer can be after ten o'clock. Saturday night dates work beautifully in winter and are brutal in June — check the havdalah time for your date before booking anything.","Friday weddings must finish with everyone home before Shabbat starts at sundown, which is why observant communities rarely use them outside winter or Israel, where a Friday afternoon chuppah before an early Shabbat-eve dinner is common.","Fully kosher catering restricts your venue as much as your menu. Many venues cannot host a supervised kosher caterer, or charge heavily for koshering the kitchen; communities keep approved lists. Decide between fully supervised kosher and 'kosher-style' before booking the venue, ","A kosher meat meal means no dairy anywhere: no butter on the table, no cream sauces, no cheese course, and a parev wedding cake. Also budget for the mashgiach (supervisor) and remember that all wine and grape juice must be kosher — with mevushal wine expected by many authorities ","The legal status of the religious ceremony varies by country. In England and Wales the chuppah can itself be legally binding after register-office notice, through the synagogue's secretary for marriages, and uniquely can be held anywhere. In the United States, Canada and Australi","In Israel there is no civil marriage. State-recognised Jewish weddings go through the Orthodox Rabbinate only, with proof of Jewish status to Orthodox standards; Reform, Conservative and secular ceremonies in Israel are not state-recognised, and mixed-faith and same-sex couples c","Proof of Jewish status can take longer than any other document. Orthodox authorisation typically wants your parents' ketubah or a letter from a recognised rabbi, and tracing records from another country — or from grandparents who fled one — can take months. Converts should raise ","A civil divorce is not a Jewish divorce. If either of you was previously married in a Jewish ceremony, Orthodox and Conservative rabbis — and the British Reform beit din — require a get before remarriage, and obtaining one can be slow, especially if the former spouse is uncoopera","The witnesses are a halachic requirement, not an honour to hand out freely. In Orthodox practice the two eidim must be observant Jewish men who are not related to either of you or to each other — which usually rules out fathers and brothers. Conservative and Reform communities ar","Interfaith couples should ask the officiating question first, not last. Orthodox and Conservative rabbis will not officiate at a marriage between a Jew and a non-Jew; policies among Reform and Liberal rabbis range from full officiation to blessings with conditions, and in Britain","The ceremony ring should be a plain band with no stones, owned outright by the giver — buying it on credit or borrowing it can technically invalidate the kiddushin in Orthodox eyes. The diamond engagement ring goes back on after the ceremony; it just isn't used under the chuppah.","If you are fasting on the wedding day, plan for it: no drinks at the kabbalat panim for the couple, a first bite in the yichud room, and a schedule that doesn't leave a fasting couple standing for photographs for three hours. Sephardi couples generally don't fast, and on certain ","The ketubah needs everyone's Hebrew names — yours and usually your parents' — with correct spellings, and the document is filled in and signed on the day. Get the names checked by your rabbi weeks early, and appoint someone to bring the ketubah, the ring and the wrapped glass to ","At an Orthodox wedding, tell the band and venue about separate dancing early: two circles need a bigger dance floor than one, and a mechitza needs setting up. Guests unfamiliar with the custom appreciate a line about it in the invitation or order of service.","An aufruf is a Shabbat event, so there is no photography and observant guests will not drive — keep it walkable and unplugged, and don't schedule the photographer.","If you are keeping the week of sheva brachot, the couple's diary is full for seven nights, which surprises couples planning a honeymoon departure the morning after the wedding. Traditional couples often delay the honeymoon a week; others hold one sheva brachot dinner and leave."]},"civil":{"id":"civil","name":"Civil / Non-religious","shortName":"Civil","faithGroup":"None","blurb":"A wedding shaped entirely by you: a registrar or celebrant, your own vows, readings and music, and no religious framework unless you choose to borrow from one.","typicalGuests":{"low":20,"typical":90,"high":180},"terms":{"officiant":"registrar or celebrant","ceremonyVenue":"ceremony room","ceremony":"ceremony","bestMan":"best man","maidOfHonour":"maid of honour","reception":"reception","firstDance":"first dance","witnesses":"witnesses"},"events":[{"id":"engagement-party","name":"Engagement party","when":"Within a few months of the engagement","optional":true,"default":false,"description":"An informal gathering to celebrate the engagement, at home, in a pub or restaurant, or as a bigger party. There is no set form; some couples use it as the moment the two families and friendship groups first mix."},{"id":"hen-stag","name":"Hen and stag celebrations","when":"One to three months before","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"Called a bachelorette and bachelor party in the US and a hen and stag do in Britain and Ireland. Increasingly mixed or joint, and anything from one night out to a weekend away. Usually organised and paid for by the wedding party rather than"},{"id":"rehearsal-walkthrough","name":"Venue walkthrough or rehearsal","when":"A day or two before, or the week of the wedding","optional":true,"default":false,"description":"Civil weddings rarely have a formal rehearsal; the registrars brief you on the day and interview each of you privately before the ceremony. Celebrant-led couples more often do a short run-through, since the celebrant controls the running or"},{"id":"statutory-marriage","name":"Statutory marriage at the register office","when":"Days or weeks before, or the same morning","optional":true,"default":false,"description":"The legal two-step for celebrant-led couples in England and Wales: a short, basic register office ceremony with just the two of you and two witnesses, which makes you legally married, followed by the celebrant ceremony everyone will remembe"},{"id":"first-look","name":"First look","when":"Wedding morning, before the ceremony","optional":true,"default":false,"description":"A private, photographed moment where you see each other before the ceremony instead of at the top of the aisle. It calms nerves, and it lets couple portraits happen before guests arrive, freeing up the drinks reception. Entirely a matter of"},{"id":"ceremony","name":"The ceremony","when":"The wedding day","optional":false,"default":true,"description":"Typically 20 to 45 minutes. A registrar-led civil ceremony includes the required legal declarations plus any non-religious readings, music and personal vows you have agreed in advance; two witnesses must be present and you sign the marriage"},{"id":"drinks-reception","name":"Drinks reception","when":"Straight after the ceremony","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"An hour or two of drinks and canapés while photographs happen and the room is turned around for the meal. At a single-venue wedding this is the natural congratulations moment, since there is no journey between ceremony and reception."},{"id":"reception","name":"Wedding breakfast and speeches","when":"Wedding day, afternoon","optional":false,"default":true,"description":"The main meal, still called the wedding breakfast in Britain whatever the hour. Speeches traditionally run father of the bride, then the groom, then the best man, but civil weddings freely reorder this: couples speak together, mothers and m"},{"id":"evening-reception","name":"Evening reception","when":"Wedding day, evening","optional":true,"default":true,"description":"The cake cutting, first dance, band or DJ, and evening food. Many British couples invite a second, larger guest list for the evening only, which lets colleagues and the wider circle join without a bigger seated meal."},{"id":"day-after-brunch","name":"Day-after gathering","when":"The day after","optional":true,"default":false,"description":"A relaxed brunch, pub lunch or barbecue for close family and guests who travelled. Common when the venue has accommodation or the wedding is a weekend away; not expected otherwise."}],"items":[{"id":"notice-fees","name":"Notice of marriage fees","section":"Ceremony","event":"","note":"The statutory fee each of you pays to give notice at the register office. In England and Wales it is charged per person and is higher if either of you is referred for immigration checks. Scotland, Nor","essential":true,"share":0.003,"supplier":"Civil registration","religious":false},{"id":"registrar-ceremony-fee","name":"Registrar and ceremony fee","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"What the registration service charges for the ceremony itself. A register office ceremony room is the cheapest route; registrars attending an approved venue cost noticeably more, and most councils pri","essential":true,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Civil registration","religious":false},{"id":"celebrant-fee","name":"Celebrant fee","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"For a celebrant-led ceremony: the fee covers meetings, writing a script from scratch around you, editing it with you, the day itself and often a rehearsal. Humanist celebrants (through Humanists UK or","essential":false,"share":0.015,"supplier":"Celebrant","religious":false},{"id":"statutory-ceremony-fee","name":"Statutory ceremony fee","section":"Ceremony","event":"statutory-marriage","note":"The basic register office ceremony that makes a celebrant-led wedding legally binding in England and Wales. It usually includes the registrars and one marriage certificate. Only needed if your main ce","essential":false,"share":0.002,"supplier":"Civil registration","religious":false},{"id":"marriage-certificates","name":"Marriage certificate copies","section":"Ceremony","event":"","note":"In England and Wales certificates are no longer handed over on the day: the signed schedule goes back to the register office, the marriage is registered electronically and certificates are posted afte","essential":true,"share":0.001,"supplier":"Civil registration","religious":false},{"id":"ceremony-room-hire","name":"Ceremony room or space hire","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"Many venues charge separately for the ceremony room or outdoor ceremony area on top of the main venue hire, and register offices charge more for their larger, prettier rooms than for the basic one. If","essential":false,"share":0.015,"supplier":"Venue","religious":false},{"id":"ceremony-musicians","name":"Ceremony musicians","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"A string quartet, acoustic guitarist, pianist or singer for the entrance, signing and exit. Entirely optional: recorded music through the venue's system is normal and most registration services simply","essential":false,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Musicians","religious":false},{"id":"unity-ceremony-supplies","name":"Symbolic ceremony supplies","section":"Ceremony","event":"ceremony","note":"The kit for any symbolic act you add: handfasting cords or ribbons, a sand-blending set, a memory or wine box, a quaich, or a tree and soil. All optional, and cheap compared with almost everything els","essential":false,"share":0.001,"supplier":"Ceremony supplies","religious":false},{"id":"destination-legal-documents","name":"Destination wedding legal documents","section":"Ceremony","event":"","note":"If you marry abroad: a certificate of no impediment where the country asks for one, apostille legalisation, certified translations, and courier fees. Requirements differ sharply by country, and some h","essential":false,"share":0.002,"supplier":"Civil registration","religious":false},{"id":"reception-venue","name":"Venue hire","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"For civil weddings the ceremony and reception are very often under one roof, which is the main structural difference from a church wedding: one venue, no travel, and in England and Wales the venue mus","essential":true,"share":0.095,"supplier":"Venue","religious":false},{"id":"catering","name":"Wedding meal","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"Usually the single largest line. Check what the per-head price actually includes: staffing, linen, cake cutting, corkage and service charge are the usual quiet extras. Dietary requirements are simpler","essential":true,"share":0.23,"supplier":"Catering","religious":false},{"id":"drinks","name":"Drinks and bar","section":"Reception","event":"reception","note":"Reception drinks, wine with the meal, a toast drink and whatever you put behind the bar. Note one rule that surprises people at approved premises in England and Wales: alcohol cannot be sold or consum","essential":true,"share":0.092,"supplier":"Catering","religious":false},{"id":"evening-food","name":"Evening food","section":"Reception","event":"evening-reception","note":"The late spread for dancers and evening guests: bacon rolls, pizza, a cheese table, a food truck. If you invite a separate evening guest list, budget this per evening head, not per daytime head.","essential":false,"share":0.02,"supplier":"Catering","religious":false},{"id":"wedding-cake","name":"Wedding cake","section":"Reception","event":"evening-reception","note":"Cut together as one of the evening's marker moments, often photographed just before the first dance. Some couples replace it with a cheese tower, doughnut wall or dessert table; nothing about a civil ","essential":true,"share":0.012,"supplier":"Cake","religious":false},{"id":"day-after-food","name":"Day-after gathering","section":"Reception","event":"day-after-brunch","note":"Brunch, a pub lunch or a barbecue for those staying over. Keep it informal and self-serve; after a wedding, nobody wants a second seating plan.","essential":false,"share":0.012,"supplier":"Catering","religious":false},{"id":"outfit-one","name":"Wedding dress or first outfit","section":"Attire","event":"","note":"No dress code comes with a civil wedding: full gown, jumpsuit, suit or something entirely your own all work. Made-to-order dresses need six months or more plus alterations; off-the-peg and second-hand","essential":true,"share":0.05,"supplier":"Bridal","religious":false},{"id":"outfit-two","name":"Partner's outfit","section":"Attire","event":"","note":"Bought or hired suit, tuxedo or a second dress. Hiring makes sense if the wedding party is matching; buying makes sense if you will wear it again.","essential":true,"share":0.018,"supplier":"Menswear","religious":false},{"id":"accessories","name":"Shoes, veil and accessories","section":"Attire","event":"","note":"Shoes, jewellery, and a veil if you want one; plenty of civil brides skip the veil entirely. If your ceremony is outdoors on grass, think about heels before the day does it for you.","essential":true,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Bridal","religious":false},{"id":"wedding-party-outfits","name":"Wedding party outfits","section":"Attire","event":"","note":"Bridesmaids, groomsmen, flower girls and page boys, if you have them; a civil wedding can just as easily have no wedding party at all. Who pays varies by family. Mismatched-but-coordinating outfits ha","essential":false,"share":0.01,"supplier":"Bridal","religious":false},{"id":"hair-makeup","name":"Hair and make-up","section":"Beauty","event":"","note":"A trial beforehand plus the wedding morning. Book the trial around the time of your final dress fitting so the whole look comes together at once.","essential":true,"share":0.012,"supplier":"Beauty","religious":false},{"id":"party-beauty","name":"Wedding party hair and make-up","section":"Beauty","event":"","note":"Priced per head for bridesmaids and mothers. Decide early whether you are treating or they are paying, and confirm numbers before the stylist books a second artist.","essential":false,"share":0.006,"supplier":"Beauty","religious":false},{"id":"bouquets-buttonholes","name":"Bouquets and buttonholes","section":"Flowers & Styling","event":"","note":"Bouquet, bridesmaids' flowers, buttonholes and corsages for parents. Dried and silk flowers are common at civil weddings and survive the day rather better than they used to.","essential":true,"share":0.014,"supplier":"Florist","religious":false},{"id":"ceremony-flowers","name":"Ceremony flowers and arch","section":"Flowers & Styling","event":"ceremony","note":"An arch, arrangement or aisle flowers to frame the ceremony spot, which matters more at a civil wedding because the backdrop is whatever you make it rather than a church. Choose pieces that can move: ","essential":false,"share":0.015,"supplier":"Florist","religious":false},{"id":"reception-flowers","name":"Reception flowers and styling","section":"Flowers & Styling","event":"reception","note":"Centrepieces, top table flowers and general styling. Because a civil wedding usually happens in one venue, one coherent look carries the whole day, which is simpler and often cheaper than dressing two","essential":true,"share":0.03,"supplier":"Florist","religious":false},{"id":"lighting-dancefloor","name":"Lighting and dance floor","section":"Flowers & Styling","event":"evening-reception","note":"Festoon lights, uplighting or a hired dance floor. Most relevant for marquees, barns and dry-hire spaces; hotels usually come dressed.","essential":false,"share":0.01,"supplier":"Styling","religious":false},{"id":"signage-decor","name":"Signage and decor extras","section":"Flowers & Styling","event":"","note":"Welcome sign, seating chart display, table names, candles and the odd neon sign. Small individually; keep a single decor list or this category multiplies quietly.","essential":false,"share":0.006,"supplier":"Styling","religious":false},{"id":"photographer","name":"Photographer","section":"Photos & Film","event":"","note":"Civil ceremonies are generally relaxed about photography, but the registrars or celebrant run the room, so have your photographer introduce themselves before the ceremony and agree where they can stan","essential":true,"share":0.068,"supplier":"Photography","religious":false},{"id":"videographer","name":"Videographer","section":"Photos & Film","event":"","note":"Highlights film or full-ceremony edit. Personal vows are the thing couples most often wish they had on film, so if the budget only stretches to partial coverage, cover the ceremony and speeches.","essential":false,"share":0.035,"supplier":"Videography","religious":false},{"id":"photo-booth","name":"Photo booth","section":"Photos & Film","event":"evening-reception","note":"A booth or backdrop with props for the evening. Doubles as a guest book if the booth prints two copies, one for guests and one for an album they sign.","essential":false,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Photography","religious":false},{"id":"band","name":"Band or main entertainment","section":"Music & Entertainment","event":"evening-reception","note":"A live band for the evening is the classic centrepiece, but civil weddings go every direction: DJ only, a ceilidh caller, an acoustic act that plays daytime and evening. Check the venue's sound limite","essential":true,"share":0.058,"supplier":"Entertainment","religious":false},{"id":"dj","name":"DJ","section":"Music & Entertainment","event":"evening-reception","note":"Either the main event or the act that takes over between and after band sets. A good DJ takes requests and reads the room; send a short do-not-play list rather than a full playlist.","essential":false,"share":0.018,"supplier":"Entertainment","religious":false},{"id":"daytime-entertainment","name":"Daytime entertainment","section":"Music & Entertainment","event":"drinks-reception","note":"Lawn games, a magician, a caricaturist or live music for the drinks reception. Most useful when portraits will take you away from guests for a stretch.","essential":false,"share":0.006,"supplier":"Entertainment","religious":false},{"id":"invitations","name":"Invitations and save the dates","section":"Stationery","event":"","note":"Printed, digital or a mix; many couples now run RSVPs through a free wedding website and print only the main invitation. If you have separate daytime and evening guest lists, you need two versions, an","essential":true,"share":0.012,"supplier":"Stationery","religious":false},{"id":"on-the-day-stationery","name":"On-the-day stationery","section":"Stationery","event":"reception","note":"Order of the day, place cards, menus and the table plan. A civil order of service is short, one card rather than a booklet, and entirely optional.","essential":false,"share":0.006,"supplier":"Stationery","religious":false},{"id":"wedding-cars","name":"Wedding cars","section":"Transport","event":"","note":"Transport to the ceremony, and between venues if you have two. At a single-venue wedding many couples skip cars entirely or hire one for the arrival photographs only.","essential":false,"share":0.01,"supplier":"Transport","religious":false},{"id":"guest-transport","name":"Guest transport","section":"Transport","event":"","note":"A coach or minibus at the end of the night is money well spent when the venue is rural, and some venues in remote spots effectively require it. Less necessary at town-centre venues near taxis and hote","essential":false,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Transport","religious":false},{"id":"wedding-rings","name":"Wedding rings","section":"Rings & Gifts","event":"ceremony","note":"Exchanged during the ceremony by long custom, though no law in the UK requires rings at all: the marriage is made by the declarations and the signatures. One ring, two rings or none are all fine, and ","essential":true,"share":0.03,"supplier":"Jewellery","religious":false},{"id":"party-gifts","name":"Gifts for the wedding party and parents","section":"Rings & Gifts","event":"","note":"Thank-yous for attendants, parents, readers and your witnesses. Witnesses are worth remembering here: at a civil wedding they have a genuine legal role, and a small gift acknowledges it.","essential":false,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Gifts","religious":false},{"id":"favours","name":"Favours","section":"Extras","event":"reception","note":"A small token at each place setting. Entirely optional and one of the first lines couples cut; a charity donation card per table is a common replacement.","essential":false,"share":0.006,"supplier":"Favours","religious":false},{"id":"wedding-insurance","name":"Wedding insurance","section":"Extras","event":"","note":"Covers supplier failure, cancellation, illness and damage. Buy it when you pay your first deposit, not the month before the wedding, because cover starts from purchase. Some venues and marquee compani","essential":false,"share":0.003,"supplier":"Insurance","religious":false},{"id":"accommodation","name":"Wedding night accommodation","section":"Extras","event":"","note":"Your room, and possibly rooms for parents or the wedding party. If the venue offers a bedroom block, find out who pays if guests do not fill it; that clause catches people out.","essential":false,"share":0.008,"supplier":"Accommodation","religious":false},{"id":"planner-coordinator","name":"Planner or on-the-day coordinator","section":"Extras","event":"","note":"A full planner suits complicated or long-distance weddings; an on-the-day coordinator suits couples who have planned everything and want to hand over the running of it. Dry-hire venues without in-hous","essential":false,"share":0.02,"supplier":"Planning","religious":false},{"id":"confetti","name":"Confetti","section":"Extras","event":"ceremony","note":"The confetti exit is one of the defining photographs of a civil wedding. Most venues insist on dried petals or other biodegradable confetti and ban the paper kind, so check the rule and tell guests, o","essential":false,"share":0.001,"supplier":"Ceremony supplies","religious":false}],"timeline":[{"mb":14,"task":"Decide the shape of the day: register office, an approved venue with a registrar, or a celebrant-led ceremony with a separate statutory marriage (England and Wales) or a legally authorised celebrant (Scotland and Northern Ireland)","critical":true},{"mb":14,"task":"Agree the overall budget and rough guest numbers with everyone contributing","critical":false},{"mb":13,"task":"In England and Wales, check the registration service's availability for your date before you confirm the venue: the registrar and the venue are separate bookings and both must line up","critical":true},{"mb":12,"task":"Book the venue, checking it is on the council's approved premises list if a registrar is marrying you there in England or Wales","critical":true},{"mb":12,"task":"Book the registrars' attendance with the council, or book your celebrant","critical":true},{"mb":12,"task":"Take out wedding insurance as soon as the first deposits are paid","critical":false},{"mb":11,"task":"Book the photographer and videographer","critical":false},{"mb":10,"task":"Book the band or DJ, checking the venue's sound limiter and finish time first","critical":false},{"mb":10,"task":"Start outfit shopping; made-to-order dresses need six months or more plus alterations","critical":false},{"mb":9,"task":"Send save the dates, especially if guests are travelling","critical":false},{"mb":8,"task":"Book the florist, and the caterer if your venue is dry hire","critical":false},{"mb":8,"task":"If marrying abroad, research that country's legal requirements, residency rules and paperwork now, and decide whether to do the legal marriage at home instead","critical":true},{"mb":7,"task":"Start shaping the ceremony: choose readings, music and any symbolic elements, and begin the script with your celebrant if you have one","critical":false},{"mb":6,"task":"In England and Wales, send your proposed music and readings to the registration service for approval, since religious content is not allowed and decisions vary between districts","critical":false},{"mb":5,"task":"Order invitations","critical":false},{"mb":4,"task":"Send invitations, making the daytime and evening guest distinction unmissable if you have one","critical":false},{"mb":4,"task":"Give notice: in England and Wales at your local register office at least 29 days before the ceremony (valid 12 months); in Scotland submit M10 forms no later than 29 days and no earlier than 3 months before; in Northern Ireland at least 28 days before","critical":true},{"mb":3,"task":"Buy the wedding rings, or decide you are not having them","critical":false},{"mb":3,"task":"Hair and make-up trial, timed near a dress fitting","critical":false},{"mb":3,"task":"Choose your two witnesses and tell them what the role involves","critical":false},{"mb":2,"task":"Write your personal vows and share them with your celebrant, or check what additions your registrar allows","critical":false},{"mb":2,"task":"Confirm final numbers with the caterer and the running order with the venue","critical":true},{"mb":1,"task":"Final venue meeting: confirm registrar or celebrant arrival time, the pre-ceremony interview, and where the signing table goes","critical":false},{"mb":1,"task":"If your main ceremony is celebrant-led in England or Wales, confirm the statutory register office booking and the witnesses attending it","critical":true},{"mb":0.5,"task":"In Scotland, collect the marriage schedule in person from the register office in the seven days before the wedding; in Northern Ireland collect it no more than 14 days before","critical":true},{"mb":0.25,"task":"Walk through the ceremony space, or hold a short rehearsal if your celebrant offers one","critical":false}],"paperwork":[{"item":"Notice of marriage (England and Wales)","who":"Both","note":"Given in person at a register office in the district where you live, after living there for at least the past 7 days. It must be given at least 29 days before the ceremony, the notice is publicly displayed at the registe"},{"item":"Documents for giving notice","who":"Both","note":"A valid passport or UK birth certificate, plus proof of address such as a driving licence, utility bill, bank statement or tenancy agreement. If you were born after 1 January 1983 and use a UK birth certificate, you may "},{"item":"Evidence a previous marriage or civil partnership has ended","who":"Either party previously married","note":"A decree absolute or final order, or the former partner's death certificate. Divorces granted outside the UK have to be checked by the registration service, which carries a fee and can add time, so raise it at the start "},{"item":"Evidence of any name change","who":"Either, if applicable","note":"A deed poll or equivalent, so the names on your documents line up."},{"item":"Immigration documents and joint notice","who":"Any partner who is not a British or Irish citizen without settled or pre-settled status","note":"You must give notice together at a designated register office, bring passport photos and evidence of immigration status, and translations of any documents not in English. The standard waiting period can be extended from "},{"item":"Marriage schedule (England and Wales)","who":"Both","note":"Since May 2021 you sign a marriage schedule at the ceremony rather than a register book. The registrar takes it away, the marriage is entered in an electronic register, and up to four parents' names (mothers included) ca"},{"item":"Marriage certificates","who":"Both","note":"No longer handed over on the day in England and Wales; they are posted after the marriage is registered. Order extra certified copies for passports, banks and name changes."},{"item":"M10 marriage notice forms (Scotland)","who":"Both","note":"Each of you submits an M10 to the registrar for the district where the ceremony will be held, no later than 29 days and no earlier than 3 months before. There is no residency requirement in Scotland; couples from anywher"},{"item":"Marriage schedule (Scotland)","who":"Both","note":"One of you must collect it in person from the register office in the seven days before the wedding; nobody can collect it for you. It is signed after the ceremony by you both, the celebrant and two witnesses aged 16 or o"},{"item":"Marriage notice forms (Northern Ireland)","who":"Both","note":"Submitted to the registrar for the district where the ceremony takes place, at least 28 days before and no more than a year ahead; registration offices recommend around 12 to 14 weeks. The schedule is collected in person"},{"item":"Three months notification (Republic of Ireland)","who":"Both","note":"You must notify a civil registrar (HSE) at least three months before the wedding, usually in person, and you receive a Marriage Registration Form that the solemniser needs on the day. Humanist Association of Ireland cele"},{"item":"Statutory ceremony booking (England and Wales two-step)","who":"Both, if your main ceremony is celebrant-led","note":"The short register office ceremony that makes the marriage legal. Councils run them on limited days with only your two witnesses attending, so book it when you book the celebrant, not as an afterthought."},{"item":"Certificate of no impediment","who":"Both, if marrying abroad and the country requires one","note":"Issued after you give notice at your local register office; only British nationals can get one this way, and other nationals should ask their embassy. Check with the country's authorities how long it stays valid, as this"},{"item":"Legalisation and translations for marrying abroad","who":"Both, if marrying abroad","note":"Many countries require your documents to be legalised with an apostille and translated by a certified translator. Allow weeks rather than days, and check whether the translations themselves need legalising."},{"item":"Foreign marriage certificate","who":"Both, if you married abroad","note":"A marriage legally performed abroad is recognised in the UK provided it followed local law and you were both free to marry; there is no requirement, and no facility, to register it in the UK afterwards. Keep the original"}],"customs":[{"name":"Personal vows","description":"Promises you write yourselves, spoken alongside the required legal declarations rather than instead of them. Registrars allow non-religious additions to the set wording; celebrants build the whole ceremony around them. Keep a printed copy each; almost nobody trusts memory on the day."},{"name":"The celebrant tells your story","description":"The heart of most celebrant-led and humanist ceremonies: the celebrant recounts how you met and who you are as a couple, gathered from meetings and questionnaires beforehand. It is the part guests most often say they remember."},{"name":"First look","description":"Seeing each other privately before the ceremony, usually photographed. A deliberate modern swap for the aisle reveal, chosen for calm nerves and better use of the day; couples who prefer the traditional moment simply skip it."},{"name":"Walking in together","description":"Many couples now enter side by side, or each walk in with a parent, or with both parents, rather than one waiting at the front. There is no rule; a civil ceremony lets you stage the entrance any way that feels honest."},{"name":"Handfasting","description":"The couple's hands are loosely bound with cords or ribbons while promises are made, a practice with Celtic roots and the likely origin of 'tying the knot'. Used in entirely secular form at many humanist and celebrant ceremonies, sometimes with family members each laying a ribbon."},{"name":"Sand blending","description":"Each of you pours coloured sand into one vessel, the layers standing for two lives combined; children from either side are often given their own colour so the whole new family pours together. A modern, secular unity ritual."},{"name":"Unity candle","description":"Two side candles used to light one central candle. It has church origins in some countries but is used in a fully secular form at civil and celebrant ceremonies; outdoor couples learn quickly that wind is its natural enemy."},{"name":"Ring warming","description":"The rings are passed among guests, or hung where guests can hold them, so everyone briefly holds them and wishes you well before the exchange. Works best at smaller ceremonies where the rings can genuinely travel the room and return in time."},{"name":"Wine box or letter ceremony","description":"You seal a bottle and letters to each other in a box during the ceremony, to be opened on a future anniversary. A celebrant-era invention with no religious content, and a favourite for couples who want a ritual object to keep."},{"name":"Tree planting","description":"You pot or plant a tree together, sometimes with soil from each family's home. Suits outdoor and garden weddings, and leaves you with something that grows; check the logistics of moving it afterwards."},{"name":"The quaich","description":"A two-handled Scottish cup shared as a first drink together, each holding one handle, as a sign of trust. Traditional at Scottish weddings and borrowed well beyond them."},{"name":"Readings by guests","description":"Friends and family read poems, prose or song lyrics during the ceremony. At a registrar-led ceremony in England and Wales the readings must be non-religious; celebrant ceremonies have no restrictions at all."},{"name":"Confetti exit","description":"Guests shower you with petals or paper shapes as you leave the ceremony or the venue. Most venues now require biodegradable confetti or dried petals, so supply it in cones and tell the photographer where the throw will happen."},{"name":"Receiving line","description":"The couple, and traditionally both sets of parents, greet every guest in turn before the meal. Falling out of fashion because it is slow at large weddings; many couples replace it by visiting tables during the meal."},{"name":"Speeches","description":"The traditional British order is father of the bride, groom, best man, ending with toasts. Civil weddings rearrange freely: both partners speak, a maid of honour speaks, parents share a toast, or speeches are dropped entirely. Tell speakers a time limit and mean it."},{"name":"First dance","description":"The couple open the dancing to a chosen song, often followed by parent dances in families that keep that custom. Couples who dread it choose a short song, invite everyone onto the floor after the first minute, or skip it without ceremony."},{"name":"Cutting the cake","description":"The couple make the first cut together, usually photographed, often just before the first dance so the evening moments run in sequence. The old custom of saving a tier has faded along with fruit cake, but nothing stops you."},{"name":"Unplugged ceremony","description":"Guests are asked to put phones and cameras away for the ceremony so the photographer's shots are clear of screens and everyone is actually watching. Announced by a sign, the celebrant or the registrar's introduction."},{"name":"Something old, something new","description":"The rhyme continues 'something borrowed, something blue', each item worn or carried for luck. A folk custom with no religious content, kept alive because it gives mothers, grandmothers and friends a way into the wedding morning."},{"name":"The night before apart","description":"Spending the last night in separate places and not seeing each other until the ceremony. Pure superstition and widely ignored by couples who would rather wake up together; the first look exists partly because this custom loosened."},{"name":"Gift lists and honeymoon funds","description":"Couples who already share a home increasingly ask for contributions to the honeymoon or a house fund instead of a traditional gift list, usually via a line on the invitation or wedding website. Cash in cards remains completely normal in many families."}],"gotchas":["In England and Wales a celebrant-led or humanist ceremony is not legally binding on its own. You also need a legal marriage before a registrar, usually a short statutory ceremony at the register office. Budget for both and put both in the diary.","The UK Government announced in October 2025 that it intends to give humanist marriages legal recognition in England and Wales, but with further consultation planned the law has not yet changed. Plan on the two-step unless your registration service confirms otherwise.","In Scotland the position is different: Humanist Society Scotland and other authorised belief celebrants have legally married couples since 2005, and in Northern Ireland humanist marriages have been legal since 2018. No separate registrar ceremony is needed there.","The registrar and the venue are separate bookings in England and Wales. Couples book a venue, announce the date, and only then discover no registrar is free. Check ceremony availability with the registration service before you pay a venue deposit.","You give notice in the district where you live, not where you are marrying, and you must have lived there for the previous 7 days. Both of you attend in person. If you live in different districts you each give notice in your own.","Notice in England and Wales is valid for only 12 months, so with a long engagement you cannot give notice when you book; the venue may be booked two years out while the legal clock cannot start until the final year.","If either of you is not a British or Irish citizen and has no settled status, you must give notice together at a designated register office, and the waiting period can be extended from 29 to 70 days for Home Office checks. Do not book a date inside that window.","A civil ceremony in England and Wales must contain nothing religious: no hymns, no scripture, and registrars vet your music and readings in advance. Songs with a passing mention of heaven or angels are treated differently from district to district, so submit your choices early ra","Outdoor ceremonies in England and Wales are legal only within the grounds of approved premises, in an outdoor area the venue has agreed with the registrar, who must consider it seemly and dignified. A beach, a park or your own back garden is not an option unless it is part of lic","A statutory register office ceremony is deliberately minimal: limited days and times, only your two witnesses present, no music, no readings, no personalisation. Treat it as the legal step, not a small wedding, and save the meaning for your main ceremony.","In England and Wales you no longer get a marriage certificate on the day. The schedule is registered electronically and certificates are posted afterwards, so do not book anything that needs proof of marriage, like a passport in a new name, for the days straight after the wedding","Witnesses must be able to see and understand what is happening, and most registration services ask that they be at least 16. Choose them in advance; pulling in a stranger works legally but makes for an odd photograph and an odder story.","At approved premises in England and Wales, alcohol cannot be sold or consumed in the ceremony area in the period immediately before and during the ceremony, so plan the welcome drinks for after the legal part, not before.","Marrying in Scotland has no residency requirement, which is why couples travel there, but the schedule must be collected in person by one of you in the seven days before the wedding and returned within three days after. For a destination-Scotland wedding that means arriving early","For a destination wedding, the country's own rules govern everything: some require weeks of residence, most require legalised and translated documents, and a certificate of no impediment takes time because it follows the notice process at home. Many couples marry legally at home ","A marriage validly performed abroad is already recognised in the UK; you cannot register it here and you cannot legally marry the same person again in the UK to be safe. Buy several certified copies of the certificate and a certified translation before leaving the country, becaus","Humanist celebrants and independent celebrants are not the same thing: humanist celebrants conduct non-religious ceremonies only, while independent celebrants will include religious or spiritual content if you ask. In Scotland and Northern Ireland, also check your celebrant is le","Registrar fees differ by day of the week and by setting: a weekend ceremony at an approved venue costs several times a weekday register office slot. If the budget is tight, a weekday or an off-season date moves this line and usually the venue price too."]}} as const;
